(window.MUSEUM_PAINT_T = window.MUSEUM_PAINT_T || {})["en"] = {"ui":{"era":"Period","era_e0":"Before 1500","era_e1":"1500–1699","era_e2":"18th century","era_e3":"19th century","era_e4":"1900 on","lead":"People have not only played games, they have painted them. Zoom in on 3,300 years of pictures and figures and look at the details.","details":"Details","links":"See or play the game","record":"About the work","game":"Game","exhibit":"Collection","room":"Room","room_chance":"Chance","hint":"Pinch or scroll to zoom · drag to move · tap a number","r_artist":"Artist","r_date":"Date","r_place":"Place","r_medium":"Medium","r_size":"Size","r_museum":"Museum","r_credit":"Credit line","r_rights":"Image","met":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","cc0":"Open Access (public domain)","metlink":"The work on the museum’s website","credit":"Images: The Metropolitan Museum of Art, The Cleveland Museum of Art, The Art Institute of Chicago and the Rijksmuseum, Open Access (public domain).","cma":"The Cleveland Museum of Art","aic":"The Art Institute of Chicago","rijks":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam"},"ex":{},"nefertari":{"title":"Queen Nefertari Playing Senet","artist":"Nina de Garis Davies (facsimile)","date":"original ca. 1290–1224 BC · facsimile 1921–22","place":"Egypt (Valley of the Queens)","game":"Senet","medium":"Tempera on paper (facsimile at half size)","size":"43 × 46 cm","credit":"Rogers Fund, 1930","p":["Nefertari was the principal queen of Ramesses II. This is a copy of a painting in her tomb in the Valley of the Queens, made inside the tomb in 1921–22; even the cracks and losses of the wall were copied.","The queen sits before a board and plays senet against an invisible opponent. Painted in a tomb, the game is thought to have meant more than play: winning the journey into the afterlife."],"spots":[["Two kinds of pieces","Pointed pieces and spool-shaped pieces stand on the board: one set for each player."],["The queen’s hand","Her fingers reach for a piece."],["Board and stand","The board is shown from the side, so the squares cannot be seen. It rests on a slender stand."],["Her name","The queen’s name is written inside an oval frame, a cartouche."],["The invisible opponent","No one sits across from her: she plays an unseen opponent."]]},"hydria":{"title":"Achilles and Ajax Playing a Board Game (water jar)","artist":"Attributed to the Leagros Group","date":"ca. 510 BC","place":"Greece (Attica)","game":"A dice game","medium":"Terracotta (black-figure)","size":"H. 54.1 cm","credit":"Fletcher Fund, 1956","p":["During the siege of Troy, two Greek heroes, Achilles and Ajax, pass the time over a board. The scene was invented around 540 BC by the potter and painter Exekias; his original survives on an amphora in the Vatican Museums.","The subject was a great favourite between about 540 and 480 BC, and more than 150 examples are known. On this jar the goddess Athena stands in the middle. The heroes play a game in which pieces move by the roll of dice."],"spots":[["Athena","The goddess Athena stands between the two."],["The hero on the left","Still holding his spear, he bends over the board."],["The hero on the right","He has not put down his weapons either: a moment’s play in the middle of a war."],["The board","The low block between them is the board."],["The shoulder","On the jar’s shoulder a chariot sets off."]]},"liubo":{"title":"Pair of Seated Figures Playing Liubo","artist":"","date":"1st century BC – 1st century AD","place":"China (Han dynasty)","game":"Liubo","medium":"Earthenware with pigment","size":"H. 35.1 cm and 34.6 cm","credit":"Charlotte C. and John C. Weber Collection, Gift of Charlotte C. and John C. Weber, 1992","p":["Two players of liubo, the craze of Han China. Figures like these were placed in tombs, carrying the wish that the pleasures of life would go on after death.","In the photograph the liubo board and bone pieces of this museum’s collection lie between them. The figures catch a dramatic moment in a heated game."],"spots":[["The liubo board","The same board as the collection’s “Liubo Board and Pieces”: see the T, L and V marks."],["The player on the left","He studies the board calmly."],["The player on the right","Hand raised, mouth wide open: has he just made a winning move?"],["Pieces on the board","Bone pieces stand at both ends of the board."],["Pieces off the board","More pieces lie on the floor."]]},"shahnama":{"title":"“Buzurjmihr Masters the Hindu Game of Chess”, from the Shahnama of Shah Tahmasp","artist":"Painting attributed to ʿAbd al-Vahhab","date":"ca. 1530–35","place":"Iran (Tabriz)","game":"Chess","medium":"Opaque watercolor, ink, silver and gold on paper","size":"Painting 24.4 × 17.5 cm (page 47.3 × 31.8 cm)","credit":"Gift of Arthur A. Houghton Jr., 1970","p":["A scene from Firdausi’s epic, the Shahnama (Book of Kings). The king of India sends an envoy with a chessboard and pieces and challenges the Persian king Nushirvan: work out how the game is played, and India will pay tribute.","Only the young sage Buzurjmihr solves it in time. In the Shahnama, Persia then sends backgammon in return, and the Indians cannot solve it. King and courtiers stare at the board and the players."],"spots":[["The chessboard","Pieces stand on a squared board, so small that their shapes are hard to make out."],["King Nushirvan","On his throne, he waits to see the riddle solved."],["Round the board","The players lean over the board."],["The verses","The panels above and below carry Firdausi’s verses."],["The courtiers","Men in turbans with tall red batons stand and watch."]]},"chaupar":{"title":"Shiva and Parvati Playing Chaupar, from a Rasamanjari series","artist":"Devidasa of Nurpur","date":"1694–95","place":"India (Basohli, Jammu)","game":"Chaupar (a relative of Ludo’s ancestor)","medium":"Opaque watercolor, ink, silver and gold on paper","size":"Image 16.5 × 27.6 cm","credit":"Gift of Dr. J. C. Burnett, 1957","p":["A page from a series illustrating the Rasamanjari, a 15th-century Sanskrit love poem. The god Shiva and his wife Parvati sit on a tiger skin around a cross-shaped board.","Shiva has just cheated Parvati out of a necklace, and she pleads with him. Bold colour, an uncertain depth of space, and colour and gesture used for their meaning: these are the marks of the Basohli school."],"spots":[["The cross-shaped board","Chaupar and pachisi are played on a board shaped like this cross."],["White bars on the board","Probably the long dice (pasa) thrown in chaupar."],["Shiva","White-bodied, with a snake round his neck."],["Parvati","Her hand held out, she asks for her necklace back."],["The tiger skin","The two sit on a tiger skin."]]},"shogi":{"title":"A Young Woman and Man Playing Shōgi (“Chūnagon Kanesuke”, from the Thirty-Six Poetic Immortals)","artist":"Suzuki Harunobu","date":"ca. 1767–69","place":"Japan (Edo)","game":"Shogi","medium":"Woodblock print; ink and color on paper","size":"28.3 × 20.6 cm","credit":"Henry L. Phillips Collection, Bequest of Henry L. Phillips, 1939","p":["A print from a series that alludes to the Thirty-Six Poetic Immortals; this one stands for the poet Chūnagon Kanesuke. On a summer night, beside a mosquito net, a young woman and man sit over a shogi board.","Harunobu was the artist who made full-colour prints, printed from many blocks, popular. Even the pieces on the board are drawn in detail."],"spots":[["A shogi board on feet","A thick board on legs, just like today’s."],["A small box","Probably the box for the pieces."],["The mosquito net","A green net hangs over the room: it is a summer night."],["The lantern","A standing lamp lights the board."],["The poem","The cloud-shaped panel carries the poet’s name and a poem."]]},"sugoroku":{"title":"Two Young Women Playing a Game of Sugoroku","artist":"Utagawa Toyokuni I","date":"Edo period (late 18th – early 19th century)","place":"Japan (Edo)","game":"Ban-sugoroku (a relative of backgammon)","medium":"Diptych of woodblock prints","size":"Each 38.1 × 25.6 cm","credit":"H. O. Havemeyer Collection, Gift of Mrs. J. Watson Webb, 1930","p":["Two prints joined into one wide picture: young women gathered in a room enjoy a game of ban-sugoroku. The game is a relative of backgammon, of the same line as the Shōsōin’s sugoroku board in this collection.","White and black stones stand on the board, and the young woman on the right holds a cup for shaking the dice. Mount Fuji shows through the window."],"spots":[["The board","White and black stones stand on its long points."],["The dice cup","The dice are shaken in this cup."],["Stones off the board","Stones already borne off, or not yet entered?"],["Mount Fuji","Fuji beyond the window."],["The join","Here the two prints meet."]]},"eakins":{"title":"The Chess Players","artist":"Thomas Eakins","date":"1876","place":"United States (Philadelphia)","game":"Chess","medium":"Oil on wood","size":"29.8 × 42.6 cm","credit":"Gift of the artist, 1881","p":["In the parlour of a Philadelphia home, two friends play chess while the artist’s father, Benjamin Eakins, looks on. Eakins learned his careful construction of space and his precise detail from his teacher in Paris, Gérôme.","In 1881 The Chess Players became the first work the Metropolitan Museum accepted as a gift from a living artist."],"spots":[["The chessmen","In the dim room, the pieces on the board stand out in the light."],["The artist’s father","Standing and watching the game: Benjamin Eakins."],["The player on the right","The painter George W. Holmes, a friend of the artist’s father."],["The drawer","A Latin inscription: “Benjamin Eakins’s son painted this in ’76.”"],["Over the mantel","A reproduction of a painting by Eakins’s teacher Gérôme."],["The cat","On the floor, only the cat seems uninterested in the game."]]},"weiqi":{"title":"Dish with immortals playing weiqi","artist":"Unknown","date":"1425–36 (Ming dynasty, Xuande period)","place":"China","game":"Go (weiqi)","medium":"Carved red and green lacquer","size":"H. 3.5, W. 14.9, L. 23.5 cm","credit":"Gift of Florence and Herbert Irving, 2015","p":["A dish of carved lacquer: dozens of coats were built up and then carved, with a green layer under the red so that the depth of the cut brings out the colours. Inside the lobed rim, immortals play go beneath a pine.","China has the legend of Lanke, the “rotten axe handle”: the woodcutter Wang Zhi watched immortals playing go in the mountains, and found that so much time had passed that his axe handle had rotted. Immortals at the board were a favourite subject. Who the standing figure is, the records do not say."],"spots":[["The board","The go board between the two immortals, carved down to its lines."],["The immortals at play","Two figures seated by the rocks around the board."],["The standing figure","A figure standing a little apart. He recalls the woodcutter of the Lanke legend, but who he is is not known."],["The moon","A round moon in the sky (or perhaps the sun)."],["The pine","A spreading pine, a tree often standing for long life and the land of the immortals."],["The peony rim","Large peonies and leaves are carved round the rim."]]},"webb":{"title":"The Backgammon Players (cabinet)","artist":"Philip Webb (designer), Edward Burne-Jones (painter), Morris, Marshall, Faulkner & Co. (maker)","date":"1861","place":"Britain (London)","game":"Backgammon","medium":"Painted pine, oil paint on leather, brass, copper","size":"H. 185.4, W. 114.3, D. 53.3 cm","credit":"Rogers Fund, 1926","p":["One of the earliest pieces of furniture from the firm William Morris founded with friends in 1861. On the leather of the doors, a man and a woman in medieval dress sit over a game of backgammon, painted in oils. It was shown at the London International Exhibition of 1862.","It sums up the Arts and Crafts aim of erasing the line between the fine and the applied arts. The background of the leather is punched with tiny dots, a medieval technique."],"spots":[["The board","The backgammon board on the table between them."],["The man","A young man, a piece in hand, studies the board."],["The woman","Facing him, a woman holding a piece (or a dice cup)."],["Flowers at their feet","Flowers bloom at the players’ feet."],["Punched leather","The leather background is punched with fine dots that catch the light."],["The lozenge bands","Bands of lozenges on gold frame the doors above and below."]]},"liberale":{"title":"The Chess Players","artist":"Liberale da Verona","date":"ca. 1475","place":"Italy (Verona)","game":"Chess","medium":"Tempera on wood","size":"34.9 × 41.3 cm","credit":"Maitland F. Griggs Collection, Bequest of Maitland F. Griggs, 1943","p":["A fragment from the front of a cassone, the decorated chest that held a Renaissance Italian bride’s belongings. Liberale was also known for illuminating the choir books of Siena Cathedral.","It shows a moment from an unidentified love story. On the companion fragment a maiden at a window seems to beckon a youth inside; here the two play chess. According to the museum, she is about to lose. Chess was a favourite figure of courtship in the love literature of the time."],"spots":[["The chessboard","A red and white board set with dark pieces; the game seems to be near its end."],["The youth","He reaches toward the board — and looks set to win."],["The maiden","Seated opposite; on the companion panel she beckons him from a window."],["The window","Beyond the open window, a hill and the sky."],["Young men watching","Men with folded arms follow the game."],["Young women watching","Women peer at the game from behind the column."]]},"mirror":{"title":"Mirror case with a couple playing chess","artist":"Unknown","date":"1325–50","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Chess","medium":"Ivory","size":"Diameter 10.2 cm","credit":"Purchase from the J. H. Wade Fund, 1940","p":["This palm-sized ivory disc once covered a hand mirror. In fourteenth-century Paris a polished metal mirror was held between two ivory plaques and carried about; carved ivories like this were luxuries prized by courtly and aristocratic owners.","Inside a curtained tent, a young man and a woman play chess. In medieval romance, chess stood for both love and war. Some have suggested the couple are Tristan and Iseult, or Lancelot and Guinevere, but no one knows. The museum asks its visitors: who do you think is winning?"],"spots":[["The chessboard","A board on a one-legged table, with a few pieces standing on it."],["The young man","He reaches across to make his next move."],["The lady","A woman in a hood; her fingertips touch a piece on the board."],["The closed hand","Her other hand is closed in her lap. Is she holding a piece she has taken?"],["The tent","Curtains tied back on both sides — a favourite setting for chess on mirror cases."]]},"morris":{"title":"A gaming board (Nine Men’s Morris)","artist":"Peter Flötner","date":"1500–46","place":"Germany","game":"Nine Men’s Morris","medium":"Woodcut","credit":"Charles W. Harkness Endowment Fund, 1923","p":["A woodcut by Peter Flötner, one of the most gifted sculptors of Renaissance Nuremberg, who also designed ornament, furniture, goldsmiths’ models and a famously satirical pack of playing cards. Hidden beneath the swirling foliage is the board of Nine Men’s Morris — “Mühle” in German: three nested squares joined at the middle of each side.","The board has 24 points. Line up three of your pieces (a mill) and you take one of your opponent’s. Prints like this also served craftsmen as patterns, and the museum showed this sheet in an exhibition of goldsmiths’ models and designs. Whether people played on the paper itself, or copied it in wood or metal, is not recorded."],"spots":[["The central square","The innermost square, filled with fine interlace."],["An outer corner","A corner of the outer square — one of the 24 points where pieces stand."],["A middle corner","The corners of the middle square are points too."],["The joining lines","Bands join the three squares at the middle of each side; pieces move along them as well."],["A mask","Scrolling cartouches with faces at the top, bottom and sides — Renaissance grotesque ornament."],["The vine border","Leaves and fruit cut out white against the black ground."]]},"leifeng":{"title":"Leifeng Pagoda board game","artist":"Unknown","date":"1736–95 (Qianlong period)","place":"China","game":"Race game","medium":"Woodblock print; ink on paper","size":"31 × 29.8 cm","credit":"Purchase from the J. H. Wade Fund, 2025","p":["A single printed sheet made to be played on, from the reign of the Qianlong emperor of Qing China. Its squares are filled with the cast of the Legend of the White Snake: the snake spirit Bai Suzhen (“White Lady”), her companion Xiaoqing (“Little Green”), the young man Xu Xuan (Xu Xian in later versions), and the monk Fahai, who at the end imprisons the White Lady beneath Leifeng Pagoda by West Lake.","The track spirals in from a square marked 起馬, “set off”, to the pagoda at the centre. Players probably raced through the story with dice, much as in the Western Game of the Goose; the rules are not recorded. According to the museum, colour prints like this were sold in the cities and hung on walls, part of urban visual culture — each colour printed from its own carved block."],"spots":[["Start","The square marked 起馬, “set off” — probably the starting point."],["White Lady and Little Green","The snake spirit Bai Suzhen and her companion Xiaoqing."],["Xu Xuan","The young man who marries the White Lady."],["The monk Fahai","The monk who imprisons her beneath the pagoda."],["Leifeng Pagoda","The pagoda by West Lake in Hangzhou, where the story ends."],["The centre","A woman seated before the pagoda and a kneeling man — the story’s last scene, and perhaps the goal."]]},"tutinama":{"title":"The wounded monkey bites the hand of the prince, his chessmate (from a Tuti-nama, Tales of a Parrot)","artist":"Dasavanta","date":"c. 1560","place":"India (Mughal court of Akbar)","game":"Chess","medium":"Gum tempera, ink and gold on paper","size":"20 × 14.2 cm (painting 10.2 × 10.6 cm)","credit":"Gift of Mrs. A. Dean Perry, 1962","p":["An illustration from the Tuti-nama, the “Tales of a Parrot”, made at the court of the Mughal emperor Akbar. It is one of the stories a mother parrot tells her chicks to warn them against friendships with other kinds of creatures, such as the fox cubs. In it, a monkey who was good at chess often played with a prince.","One day, with guests present, the monkey made an unkind joke and the prince slapped him — and the monkey bit the prince’s hand. Outside on the right, servants prepare food while the astonished guests look on. An inscription at the bottom names Dasavanta, who later became one of the most celebrated masters at the Mughal court; as a young artist he laid a cool blue layer under his painting."],"spots":[["The monkey","Biting the prince’s hand — his partner at chess."],["The prince","The prince whose hand is bitten."],["The chessboard","A red-squared board on the floor."],["The astonished guests","The guests invited to the gathering."],["Preparing food","Servants outside, setting pots on the fire."],["The Persian text","Above, the story is written out in Persian."]]},"teniers":{"title":"Game of Backgammon","artist":"David Teniers","date":"1640s","place":"Flanders","game":"Backgammon","medium":"Oil on wood, transferred to canvas","size":"59 × 80.6 cm","credit":"Gift of Mr. and Mrs. Samuel D. Wise, 1943","p":["A high-stakes game of backgammon by the Flemish painter Teniers. Backgammon is very old; its modern form appeared in the 1400s, and the name “backgammon” was coined at about the time this picture was painted. In the 1600s the game was hugely popular and often played for money.","Onlookers crowd around the two players, and against the wall someone keeps score. From the women watching — one leans from a window — the museum takes the room to be a gaming room in a brothel. Scenes of play like this often carried a moral: gambling was foolish, even wicked, and a waste of time."],"spots":[["The board","An open backgammon board on the table, the game under way."],["The man in white","A player seen from behind, reaching for his men."],["The man with his chin in his hand","The opponent, deep in thought."],["Keeping score","A man marking a board on the wall — the score, or the stakes?"],["The woman at the window","An older woman watching from above."],["The back room","Men drink beside a young woman in the room beyond."]]},"imari":{"title":"Dish with two men playing go","artist":"Unknown","date":"1700s","place":"Japan (Edo period)","game":"Go","medium":"Porcelain, molded, with overglaze enamel (Hizen ware, Imari style)","size":"2.4 × 13.8 cm","credit":"Severance and Greta Millikin Collection, 1964","p":["On this small square dish, two men play go under a pine tree: one in yellow sits facing the board, set straight on the ground, while his opponent in green leans back against the tree.","It is Imari porcelain from Hizen (today’s Saga and Nagasaki), and its greens recall Kutani enamels. It was made for export, after Japan had replaced China as Europe’s main supplier of porcelain."],"spots":[["The go board","A board ruled in squares, set straight on the ground."],["The man in yellow","Seated, facing the board."],["The man in green","His opponent, leaning back against the tree and smiling."],["The pine","A pine stretches its branches overhead."],["The red frame","A red frame with cut corners surrounds the picture."]]},"shoei":{"title":"The Four Accomplishments (one of a pair of screens)","artist":"attributed to Kano Shōei","date":"late 1500s–early 1600s","place":"Japan (Momoyama period)","game":"Go","medium":"One of a pair of six-panel folding screens; ink and slight color on paper","size":"153 × 358.6 cm","credit":"John L. Severance Fund, 1979","p":["A six-panel screen attributed to Kano Shōei of the Kano school. With its partner it shows the “Four Accomplishments” expected of a cultured Chinese gentleman — playing the qin, playing qi (go), calligraphy and painting. Here elite Chinese gentlemen and their servants relax in the countryside beneath the pines.","At the centre two men play go while a third watches. Nearby a table holds books and a qin, still in its cloth case, waiting to be played. The scene balances enjoyment and formality. The museum points out that one of the servants is distracted by the movement of a fish in the water."],"spots":[["The go board","A footed board; two gentlemen play across it."],["The gentlemen","Two players and a third who watches."],["The table","Probably the table with books and the qin still in its case."],["The servants","Attendants waiting by the table."],["The distracted servant","Lying by the water, watching a fish move."],["The pine","A great pine spreads its branches over the scene."]]},"umaya":{"title":"Horse Stable (go and shogi on the veranda)","artist":"Unknown","date":"Early 1500s","place":"Japan (Muromachi period)","game":"Go and shogi","medium":"One of a pair of six-panel folding screens; ink, color and gold on paper","size":"146.1 × 346.6 cm","credit":"Edward L. Whittemore Fund, 1934","p":["This folding screen of six joined panels is one of a pair. Prized horses in their stalls may be the main theme, but a lot is happening on the veranda in front.","There, people challenge one another at two different board games: go on the left and shogi on the right. As the museum puts it, the lively mood comes from the interplay of the restless horses and the quiet strategy of the games. In Japan monkeys were thought to protect horses, and were sometimes kept in stables."],"spots":[["The go board","A finely ruled board with black stones on it."],["Two men in black caps","Men in tall black eboshi caps: the one in green reaches for the board, the one in red looks on."],["The shogi board","A board with large squares — far fewer than on the go board."],["The laughing opponent","A shaven-headed monk picks up a piece while the man opposite clutches his head and roars with laughter. Has someone just scored a point?"],["A monkey","At the end of the veranda — a monkey in a red coat?"],["A horse","A black horse rears up, held by its ropes."]]},"goryeo":{"title":"Pitcher with inlaid figures playing go","artist":"Unknown","date":"1200s","place":"Korea (Goryeo dynasty)","game":"Go (baduk)","medium":"Celadon ware with inlaid white and black slip","size":"H. 20.4 cm, diam. 14.3 cm","credit":"Gift of John L. Severance, 1917","p":["A Goryeo celadon pitcher, with a spout and a handle. Inlay — carving lines into the clay, filling them with white and black slip, then glazing and firing — was the specialty of Goryeo potters, and here two figures sitting under a willow stand out in white inside a round frame.","Between them is a board, and the museum compares the scene to pictures of playing go — baduk in Korean. Playing the zither, go, calligraphy and painting were the “four accomplishments” of the cultivated scholar, and their elegant gatherings became designs for craftsmen too. Korean inlaid celadon showing people is rare."],"spots":[["The board","The board between the two, shown as a white square."],["The figure on the left","Seated, facing the board."],["The figure on the right","The opponent, sitting across from him."],["The willow","Willow branches hang over them, drawn in black slip."],["The cloud frame","Swirling cloud-like scrolls run around the round frame."],["The crackle","Fine cracks in the glaze. They form as the pot cools, because clay and glaze shrink by different amounts."]]},"kangra":{"title":"Couple playing chaupar on a terrace","artist":"Unknown","date":"c. 1790–1800","place":"India (Pahari, Kangra school)","game":"Chaupar","medium":"Drawing with red ochre underdrawing","size":"19.4 × 13.2 cm","credit":"Edward L. Whittemore Fund, 1936","p":["A drawing by an artist of Kangra, in the Pahari hills at the foot of the Himalayas. The first sketch was made in red (red ochre) and then refined in black. With no color laid over it, every line of the figures and their things can be followed.","A man and a woman sit on a terrace on either side of a cross-shaped board, playing chaupar — an old Indian race game on the same cross as pachisi, moved by throwing long dice (pasa). The squares have not yet been drawn on the board. Attendants wait on the pair."],"spots":[["The cross-shaped board","A board in the shape of a cross, on the floor. Its squares are not drawn yet."],["The woman","Her palm turned up — about to throw the dice?"],["The man","A turban with a plume; he reaches toward the board."],["The attendant on the left","She holds a small flask and stretches out a cloth."],["The attendant on the right","She holds a long-handled implement and a small vessel."],["The red underdrawing","Around the turban, the red first sketch shows beneath the black lines."]]},"vuillard":{"title":"The Game of Checkers (from Landscapes and Interiors)","artist":"Édouard Vuillard","date":"1899","place":"France","game":"Checkers","medium":"Color lithograph (state III/III); printed by Auguste Clot, published by Ambroise Vollard","size":"Image 33 × 25.7 cm (sheet 38 × 30.8 cm)","credit":"Gift of the Hanna Fund, 1951","p":["Ambroise Vollard, a dealer in Impressionist paintings, was also an important publisher of prints. In 1895 he commissioned a portfolio of 12 color lithographs from Pierre Bonnard, and many more albums followed — among them Vuillard’s Landscapes and Interiors, printed by the master printer Auguste Clot.","Bonnard and Vuillard belonged to the Nabis — Hebrew for “prophets” — who sought a new art in which form and color themselves carried feeling, and who loved scenes of city life and middle-class home life. In a room crowded with pattern, two men face each other over a checkers board while a woman stands and watches. In French, checkers is “dames”; the pencil note at lower left reads “partie de dames”, a game of checkers."],"spots":[["The board","A small checkered board, with the pieces set out on it."],["The man on the left","A bearded man reaches out over the board."],["The man on the right","His opponent, bent over the board in an armchair, half hidden by the standing woman."],["The standing woman","Hand to her chin, she follows the game."],["The lamp","A lamp by the wall. Wallpaper and upholstery alike are full of pattern."],["The pencil note","At lower left, in pencil: “partie de dames” — a game of checkers."]]},"talbot":{"title":"The Chess Players (early photograph)","artist":"Attributed to William Henry Fox Talbot","date":"c. 1843–47","place":"England","game":"Chess","medium":"Salted paper print","size":"19.9 × 15 cm","credit":"Purchased with funds provided by Mr. and Mrs. Everett Kovler","p":["The negative-positive process invented by William Henry Fox Talbot, with the endless copies it made possible, dominated photography until the digital age. His early experiments show both his interest in science and his artistic aspirations, and scholars have confirmed that he took ten or more views of chess players.","Yet this one may not be his. This print and other versions of the same scene are unsigned and on a different paper from Talbot’s usual stock. The sitters are probably the photographer Antoine Claudet and Talbot’s assistant Nicolaas Henneman; recent research suggests the pictures were made in Claudet’s studio, passed to Henneman, and reached Talbot when Henneman gave him prints toward a debt (the museum’s note)."],"spots":[["The board","The chessboard on the table, its pieces lined up."],["The man in the top hat","Hat still on, he reaches for a piece."],["The thinker","Hand at his chin, his opponent studies the board."],["The curtain","A curtain hangs behind — a backdrop for the picture?"],["Another hat","A second top hat sits on the stand at right."]]},"pvl":{"title":"Men Playing a Game of Dice","artist":"Master PVL","date":"c. 1520","place":"Netherlands","game":"Dice","medium":"Engraving on paper","size":"9.4 × 9.4 cm (round)","credit":"Charles Hack and the Hearn Family Trust Collection, purchased 2023","p":["The work of an unnamed engraver active in the Netherlands in the early 1500s, known as Master PVL from the monogram “PVL” cut at lower left. On a round print small enough to sit in the palm of a hand, dense fine lines build the depth of the room and the feel of the clothes.","Two dice lie on the table. A man leaning in and a youth with a fur collar point and argue — over the count, perhaps. Women look on from behind, and a jug of wine stands under the table. Dice have been tied to gambling since antiquity, and were banned again and again."],"spots":[["The dice","Two dice tumbling on the table."],["Pointing hands","Two fingers meet on the table — an argument over the count?"],["The man in the broad hat","Leaning in. His slashed, puffed sleeves were the fashion of the day."],["The youth with the fur collar","A youth in a great hat and a fur collar."],["The women watching","Women peer at the game from behind."],["The monogram","“PVL” at lower left — the only clue to the artist’s name."]]},"kiyohiro":{"title":"Sugoroku Players","artist":"Torii Kiyohiro","date":"c. 1750","place":"Japan (Edo period)","game":"Ban-sugoroku","medium":"Color woodblock print (benizuri-e), ōban; published by Urokogataya","size":"43.5 × 29.3 cm","credit":"Clarence Buckingham Collection","p":["Torii Kiyohiro was a ukiyo-e artist active from about 1737 to 1776. This is a benizuri-e, printed from a few color blocks — pink beni, green and the like — in the years before full-color nishiki-e prints appeared in 1765.","The game on the black stand is ban-sugoroku: two players throw dice and move white and black pieces, and the first to bear all their pieces off wins. It belongs to the same family as backgammon, and ukiyo-e prints often show women enjoying it. Behind them a woman smokes a long pipe and watches."],"spots":[["The board","A sugoroku board on its stand, with white and black pieces."],["The player on the left","Reaching out to move a piece."],["The woman on the right","Bent over the board, the opponent studies the pieces."],["The woman with the pipe","With a long kiseru pipe, she follows the game from behind."],["The raised hand","The woman on the right holds up something small and black — the dice cup?"],["The signature","Signed “Torii Kiyohiro hitsu”, with the seal of the publisher Urokogataya."]]},"ingram":{"title":"The Game of Chinese Chess","artist":"John Ingram, after François Boucher","date":"1741–63","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Xiangqi","medium":"Etching and engraving","size":"Sheet 39.4 × 27.7 cm (plate 24.5 × 16.4 cm)","credit":"The Elisha Whittelsey Collection, 1966","p":["A drawing by the French painter François Boucher, engraved by the London-born printmaker John Ingram. Ingram moved to Paris and, between 1741 and 1763, made a series of prints after Boucher’s chinoiserie — China as Europeans imagined it. The title below reads “LE JEU D'ECHETS CHINOIS”, the game of Chinese chess.","A woman with her hair piled high moves a piece on the board in her lap while a small child looks on. The game is taken to be xiangqi, yet real xiangqi pieces are all flat discs, while here tall pieces stand beside little square ones. The palm tree and the figure seated on the urn, too, belong less to China than to the faraway land that Europe dreamed of at the time."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","The board on her lap, with tall pieces and square ones. Real xiangqi pieces are flat discs."],["The hand at a piece","Her right hand moves a piece."],["Chin on hand","Her left hand at her chin, she looks down at the board."],["The watching child","A child stands by the board, following the game."],["The figure on the urn","A figure sits cross-legged on top of a great urn."],["Title and names","“Dessiné par Fr. Boucher”, “Gravé par Ingram” and the title “LE JEU D'ECHETS CHINOIS”."]]},"posada":{"title":"Game of the Goose","artist":"José Guadalupe Posada","date":"1909","place":"Mexico","game":"Game of the Goose","medium":"Relief engraving on zinc on paper","size":"29.3 × 39.4 cm","credit":"William McCallin McKee Memorial Endowment","p":["A single sheet to play on, made by the Mexican printmaker Posada — famous for his skeleton calaveras — for the Mexico City publisher Antonio Vanegas Arroyo. The banner at lower left reads “JUEGO DE LA OCA” (game of the goose), and the track spirals from square 1 to 63 at the centre.","The rules (EXPLICACION) are printed in the middle: a first throw of 5 and 4 goes to 26, of 6 and 3 to 53. Land on a goose (a bird) and you count the same again; at the inn (Venta, 19) you wait two turns; in the well (Poso, 31) and the prison (Carcel, 52) you stay until someone else comes; the labyrinth (42) sends you back to 39, and death (Muerte, 58) back to the start. You must land exactly on 63. Players also paid stakes when they landed on certain squares — it was a betting game too."],"spots":[["The rules","The “EXPLICACION” in the middle: what each square does, and when to pay a stake."],["Square 1","The start. From here the track spirals in toward the centre."],["The well (31)","A well like a fountain. Fall in and you stay until someone else comes."],["Death (58)","The skull square. Land here and you go back to the start."],["The title banner","“JUEGO DE LA OCA” on the banner at lower left, with children at play beneath it."],["The publisher’s name","The goddess’s banner reads “IMPRENTA DE A. VANEGAS ARROYO”, the house that published many of Posada’s prints."]]},"jintile":{"title":"Tile with Women Playing a Board Game","artist":"Unknown","date":"1200s","place":"China (Jin dynasty)","game":"A board game (probably go)","medium":"Earthenware (molded relief)","size":"H. 27.3 cm, W. 24.8 cm, D. 5.4 cm","credit":"Purchase, Friends of Asian Art Gifts, 2002","p":["A relief tile made under the Jin (1115–1234), the dynasty of the Jurchen people who ruled north China. Under a flowering tree, two women sit facing each other on a platform with a board between them.","The board is cut with a fine grid, so they are probably playing go. The woman on the right leans in and points at the board while the one on the left watches. Many Jin-period tombs in north China were lined with bricks carved in relief with scenes of daily life, and this tile probably decorated such a tomb."],"spots":[["The board","A finely ruled board — probably a go board."],["The pointing hand","The woman on the right points at the board. Her next move?"],["The woman on the left","Hair piled high, she watches the board."],["The woman on the right","Leaning in over the board."],["The flowering tree","Branches of a flowering tree spread over them."],["The platform","The platform they sit on, with a scalloped apron between its legs."]]},"kyosai":{"title":"Farting Upsets a Game of Go (from a sketch album)","artist":"Kawanabe Kyōsai","date":"Mid- to late 1800s","place":"Japan (Edo to Meiji period)","game":"Go","medium":"One opening of an accordion-style album of 26 double leaves; ink and color on paper","size":"Album (closed) 28 × 18 cm","credit":"Given by Anne and Anthony d’Offay in honor of Evan Turner","p":["A scene from a sketch album by Kawanabe Kyōsai (1831–1889), a painter active from the last years of the shoguns into the Meiji era, drawn in quick, free brushstrokes on the pages of a folding album. Trained in the Kanō school’s exacting technique, Kyōsai was also loved for his demons, caricatures and satire.","The museum’s title says it all: “Farting Upsets a Game of Go.” On the left a man in a tall black cap bends over; on the right the go board flies through the air, the stone bowls rain stones, and his opponent is knocked flat on his back. A serious game ruined in a single blast — Kyōsai sets the old Japanese tradition of fart humor, already found in medieval scrolls, down with his rapid brush."],"spots":[["The man in the black cap","Bent over, his backside thrust out — the cause of the trouble?"],["The flying board","The ruled go board sails off at an angle."],["The bowls and stones","Two stone bowls tumble, and the stones pour down."],["The fan","An open fan is blown away beside the board."],["The toppled opponent","Flat on his back, eyes wide."],["The brushwork","Strong ink lines, as if drawn in one go without a sketch."]]},"yokuts":{"title":"Gambling Tray","artist":"Yokuts weaver (name unknown)","date":"c. 1900","place":"United States (near Dunlap, California)","game":"Huuchuish (walnut-shell dice)","medium":"Plant fibers (coiled)","size":"H. 5.1 cm, diam. 61 cm","credit":"Purchased with funds provided by Mrs. Leonard S. Florsheim, Jr.","p":["Many of California’s Indigenous peoples played games of chance. This tray was used for huuchuish, a dice game played only by women: eight walnut-shell dice were thrown onto the tray, and sticks were used to keep score.","The circular design woven into the tray shows the game itself — the dice, the score sticks and the players (the museum’s note). It is a piece of equipment that carries a picture of its own game. In the photograph, walnut-shell dice lie on and around the tray."],"spots":[["The players","Figures circle the rim — the people who join the game."],["Score sticks","Bundles of short upright lines: the sticks used to keep score."],["Round motifs","Small round motifs — the dice, perhaps?"],["A walnut die","A walnut-shell die. Eight were thrown onto the tray."],["The band of triangles","A band of dark triangles runs all the way round."],["The centre","The coil spirals out from the middle, ringed by a dashed circle."]]},"hokkei":{"title":"Ushiwakamaru Defeats Benkei at Sugoroku","artist":"Totoya Hokkei","date":"c. 1825","place":"Japan (Edo period)","game":"Ban-sugoroku","medium":"Surimono (color woodblock print, shikishiban)","size":"21.8 × 19.1 cm","credit":"Gift of Helen C. Gunsaulus","p":["A surimono by Totoya Hokkei (1780–1850), a pupil of Hokusai. Surimono were prints privately commissioned — often by poetry circles, with their own verses — and finished more lavishly than commercial ukiyo-e, with fine paper and metallic pigments.","Ushiwakamaru, the boyhood name of the hero Minamoto no Yoshitsune, and the giant warrior-monk Benkei. Their famous meeting was a duel on Kyoto’s Gojō Bridge, but here the contest becomes a game of sugoroku: the boy sits calmly beyond the board while Benkei, his halberd on his back, scratches his head in defeat — a playful re-staging, it seems."],"spots":[["The sugoroku board","A box-shaped board with white and black pieces."],["Benkei scratching his head","In full armour, the beaten Benkei scratches his head."],["Ushiwakamaru","The boy hero, seated beyond the board."],["The halberd","The blade of Benkei’s great naginata."],["The poem","Verses at upper right — probably by the circle that commissioned the print."],["The signature","Hokkei’s signature in the cartouche at lower right."]]},"vorsterman":{"title":"Backgammon Players (a night scene)","artist":"Lucas Emil Vorsterman, after Adam de Coster","date":"c. 1630","place":"Flanders (Antwerp)","game":"Backgammon","medium":"Engraving on ivory paper","size":"Image 25.5 × 34.4 cm","credit":"Bernard F. Rogers Collection","p":["Adam de Coster was a leading painter of candlelit night scenes in Antwerp around 1630, when Vorsterman, in the same city, was engraving works after popular contemporaries. A pupil of Rubens, Vorsterman fell out with his master and went to England, after which he made stronger, more stylized, Caravaggesque engravings like this one.","Around a table in a dark room, men gather over a backgammon board. Following De Coster’s handling of light, two candle flames light up the faces around the table (the museum’s note). On the right a young lute player looks out at us."],"spots":[["The board","A backgammon board opened like a box on the table."],["The candle","A candle in its holder; its flame lights the faces."],["The man with the pipe","In a broad hat, he smokes and watches the game."],["The man from behind","Leaning over, hands on the table."],["The lute player","A youth with a lute, smiling out at us."],["The names","“A. De Coster pi.” at lower left, “Vorsterman” at lower right."]]},"zorn":{"title":"Chess-Players","artist":"Anders Zorn","date":"1891","place":"Sweden","game":"Chess","medium":"Etching on ivory laid paper","size":"Image 13.5 × 9.6 cm (plate 13.9 × 10 cm)","credit":"Wallace L. DeWolf and Charles Deering Collections of Etchings by Anders Zorn","p":["An etching by the Swedish artist Anders Zorn (1860–1920), who won international fame for his portraits in oil and watercolour and also produced a large body of etchings. The plate is no bigger than a hand.","Fast, slanting lines laid one over another are all it takes to bring a dark room and two people out of the copper. The man in front, his back to us, leans over the board; the figure opposite looks down, thinking about the next move. The pieces are drawn on the board in thin strokes. Who sat for it is not recorded."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","The light area below is the board; the short upright strokes are pieces."],["The man in front","His back to us, he bends over the board."],["The opponent","Head bowed, eyes on the board."],["The dark background","The depths of the room, built of upright lines."],["Slanting lines","The coat is made of quick diagonal strokes laid over each other."],["The signature","Signed “Zorn” in pencil below the plate."]]},"harunobu-sugoroku":{"title":"Watching the Game (sugoroku)","artist":"Suzuki Harunobu","date":"c. 1766","place":"Japan (Edo period)","game":"Ban-sugoroku","medium":"Color woodblock print (chūban, surimono)","size":"28.5 × 21 cm","credit":"Clarence Buckingham Collection","p":["Suzuki Harunobu (1725?–1770) was the central figure when full-color nishiki-e prints, printed from many blocks, appeared around 1765. This is a privately issued surimono, and the soft, layered colors show what was new.","In a room with plum blossoms, two young women play at a box-shaped sugoroku board while a third stands and looks down at it. White and black pieces line the board; the girl on the left moves a piece and the one on the right reaches for the board. The sliding door behind is painted with a waterfall and pines."],"spots":[["The sugoroku board","A box-shaped ban-sugoroku board with white and black pieces."],["The girl on the left","In a kimono patterned with paper cranes, moving a piece."],["The girl on the right","Bending forward, reaching for the board."],["The onlooker","Standing, she peers at the game, holding a long-handled object."],["The plum","A white plum stretches its branches from above."],["The painted door","A sliding door painted with a waterfall and pines."]]},"kota":{"title":"Maharao Kishor Singh II of Kota Worshiping Krishna as Brij Rajji","artist":"Unknown","date":"c. 1825","place":"India (Rajasthan, Kota)","game":"Pachisi","medium":"Gum tempera, silver and gold on paper","size":"24.5 × 18.8 cm","credit":"James Albert and Mary Gardiner Ford Memorial Fund","p":["The ruler of the small princely state of Kota (r. 1819–28), dressed as a priest of the Pushti Marg sect, offers a lamp with five flames. In the little shrine on the altar stand metal images of Krishna flanked by two consorts — an icon worshipped only in Kota.","Many glass lamps hang from the ceiling, and on the steps up to the altar are toys and games to amuse Krishna and his companions: brass elephants, silver cows, balls and a pachisi board (the museum’s note). The red cross-shaped cloth is that board — pachisi set out as a plaything for the god."],"spots":[["The pachisi board","The red cross-shaped cloth on the steps: a pachisi board."],["Elephants","Two brass elephants stand beside the board."],["Balls","Golden balls on the step above."],["Krishna","The images of Krishna and two consorts in the little shrine."],["The ruler and his lamp","Dressed as a priest, the ruler offers a lamp with five flames."],["Glass lamps","A host of glass lamps hangs from the ceiling."]]},"coyote":{"title":"Game of the Coyote","artist":"Follower of José Guadalupe Posada","date":"Undated","place":"Mexico","game":"Coyote and hens (a fox-and-geese game)","medium":"Relief engraving on zinc on paper","size":"38.2 × 30.5 cm","credit":"William McCallin McKee Memorial Endowment","p":["A single sheet to play on, printed from zinc relief like the sheets Posada made for Mexico City’s popular presses. Across the top runs “JUEGO DEL COYOTE” (game of the coyote); around the board, coyotes raid the henhouse and a man swings a stick to drive one off. The strip below reads “Property of Ildefonso T. Orellana.”","The rules are printed in the “EXPLICACION” at the bottom: two players, one with fifteen hens and the other with a single coyote. Hens move forward or sideways but never back; the coyote moves in every direction and eats a hen by jumping over it when the point behind is empty. The hens win by hemming the coyote in. It is the same hunt game as Europe’s fox and geese."],"spots":[["The coyote’s point","The coyote’s head in the central circle: where the coyote starts."],["The hens’ points","Circles with hens: where the hen pieces are set out."],["The rules","The “EXPLICACION”: pieces, moves, and how to win."],["The title","“JUEGO DEL COYOTE” — game of the coyote."],["A coyote after a hen","At lower left, a coyote goes for a hen."],["The man with the stick","At upper right, a man raises a stick to chase off a coyote."]]},"mons":{"title":"Trictrac for Mons (Bergen), 1709","artist":"Pieter Nolpe (plate and publisher)","date":"Plate 1623–53; printed c. 1709","place":"Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Trictrac (a backgammon game)","medium":"Etching, engraving and letterpress verse","size":"Sheet 35.8 × 18.6 cm (plate 12.9 × 17.5 cm)","credit":"","p":["Four men play trictrac at a table. According to the museum’s record, the letters A–D mark the Duke of Marlborough, the Elector of Bavaria, the Lord of Ouwerkerk and the Duke of Villeroy. The print is a satire on the Allies’ siege and capture of Mons (Bergen in Dutch) from the French in 1709, shown as a game of tables played for the town. In the room behind (E), King Philip V of Spain is with a woman.","The plate is signed by Pieter Nolpe as maker and publisher, but Nolpe died in the early 1650s — so an old 17th-century plate was probably reprinted with the letters and a new verse added. The verse below opens by saying that the game of great lords is all tiktak and verkeer (two tables games), then mocks each player in turn."],"spots":[["The board","The trictrac board on the table: a game played for a town."],["A, Marlborough","Standing, looking down at the board — the English general of the Allies."],["B, the Elector of Bavaria","Bareheaded, slumped back in his chair."],["C, Ouwerkerk","In a plumed hat: the Dutch general."],["E, the room behind","Through the doorway, Philip V with a woman in the bedroom."],["The verse","Dutch verse printed below, mocking the players A to E."]]},"stembus":{"title":"The Ballot Box: A Political Game of the Goose","artist":"Johan Michaël Schmidt Crans (printmaker)","date":"1867","place":"Netherlands","game":"Game of the Goose (satire)","medium":"Lithograph","size":"66 × 50 cm","credit":"","p":["A satirical print in the form of a game of the goose. It came with the weekly De Nederlandsche Spectator, no. 9, 2 March 1867, together with a loose sheet of rules (the museum’s record). Portraits of the ministers of the day fill the squares.","The game starts at the Binnenhof, the parliament in The Hague, and ends in the middle at square 64, “Insulinde” — a poetic name for the Dutch East Indies — where a painted portrait of the minister Pieter Mijer hangs from the island of Java. Mijer had gone from Minister of Colonies to Governor-General of the Indies, and the appointment set off a political storm. The extra square, one more than the usual 63, is probably there for the punchline."],"spots":[["The start","The building at top left: the game begins at the Binnenhof in The Hague."],["Mijer’s portrait","Square 63: a framed portrait of Pieter Mijer, hung by ropes from the island."],["Insulinde 64","The island and the number 64 in the middle: the finish."],["The ballot box","“DE STEMBUS” (the ballot box) at lower right — the title’s box."],["The ministers","The faces of the ministers of the day look out of the squares."],["The rooster","A rooster in one square; what it mocks is not known."]]},"koorai":{"title":"Koreans at a Board Game (from Siebold’s Nippon)","artist":"James Erxleben (lithographer), after a design by ‘Toioske’","date":"1832–52","place":"Netherlands (Leiden) / people of Korea","game":"A board game (probably go)","medium":"Tinted lithograph, hand-coloured","size":"Sheet 38 × 55.5 cm","credit":"Gift of F. G. Waller","p":["A plate from NIPPON, the great work that the German-born physician Philipp Franz von Siebold published in Leiden from what he had seen at the Dutch trading post in Nagasaki (1823–30). It is marked “TAB. I” at upper right and “KOORAI” (Korea) below, with “a fisherman’s family” in Dutch and German. Erxleben drew it on the stone; the plate names the designer as “Toioske”.","Four men sit on the ground round a board; on the left a woman leads a small child, on the right a man stands with a long stick and a pouch. A broad-brimmed black hat (gat) lies in front. The board shows a grid and round stones, so it is probably go (baduk in Korean), though the record says only “a board game”."],"spots":[["The board","A grid with round stones — probably a game of go (baduk)."],["The black hat","A broad-brimmed black hat (gat) on the ground."],["Mother and child","A woman leading a child, watching the game."],["The standing man","A man standing with a long stick and a pouch."],["The man bending over","In a blue jacket, he peers at the board."],["The title","“KOORAI” (Korea), with “a fisherman’s family” in Dutch and German."]]},"cairo":{"title":"People Round a Board Game outside a Café (Cairo)","artist":"Délié & Béchard (Hippolyte Délié and Émile Béchard)","date":"c. 1869–90","place":"Egypt (Cairo)","game":"A board game (probably chess)","medium":"Albumen print","size":"10.8 × 16.4 cm","credit":"","p":["A photograph by Hippolyte Délié and Émile Béchard, French photographers who kept a studio in Cairo. The mount is printed with the studio’s name, “by the Ezbekiyya Garden”, and “Cairo (Egypt)”. Pictures like this were bought by travellers as souvenirs of Egypt.","Men sit on the ground before a shopfront with a latticed window, gathered round a board on a low stand. Tall pieces stand on the board, so it is probably chess (shatranj in Arabic), though the museum’s record says only “a board game”. Everyone holds still for the long exposure."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","The board on its low stand, with tall pieces: probably chess."],["The latticed window","A window fitted with wooden lattice, typical of old Cairo houses."],["The man with the stick","Seated with a stick, watching the game."],["The basket","A woven basket on the ground."],["The watching child","A child peers at the board from behind."],["The studio’s name","“AU JARDIN DE L’ESBÉKIEH” and “CAIRE (EGYPTE)” on the mount."]]},"vanmour":{"title":"Party of Armenians Playing Cards","artist":"Jean-Baptiste Vanmour","date":"c. 1720–37","place":"Ottoman Empire (Istanbul)","game":"Cards","medium":"Oil on canvas","size":"44.5 × 58.5 cm","credit":"","p":["Born in Flanders, Vanmour went to Istanbul in 1699 with the French ambassador and lived in the city until his death, painting court ceremonies and the daily life of its people. The museum notes that the men’s clothing shows them to be Armenians.","By the light of a large candle, men and women sit round a table spread with red cloth, playing cards. Cards lie dealt on the table, and some study the hands they hold. In the left corner an old woman is spinning. The record does not say which game it is."],"spots":[["Cards on the table","Cards laid on the red-covered table."],["The large candle","A single candle lights the whole party."],["The old woman spinning","An old woman spins in the left corner."],["A woman with her hand","At the far right, a woman studies her cards."],["The man in fur","A man in a fur-trimmed coat; the clothes mark the men as Armenians."],["Reaching across","A figure leans over the table, cards in hand."]]},"algiers":{"title":"Three Men Playing a Board Game (Algiers)","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1851–1900","place":"Algeria (Algiers)","game":"A board game (probably backgammon)","medium":"Albumen print","size":"14.0 × 10.0 cm","credit":"","p":["An anonymous photograph inscribed “Alger” (Algiers) by hand below the image. With chairs and a small table set before a plain backdrop, it was probably taken in a studio. Photographs of North African people like this were bought by travellers and collectors.","Two men face each other across a rimmed board, and the man on the right is reaching for a piece. Round pieces are stacked on the board, so it is probably backgammon (known in the region as tawla and other names), though the record says only “a board game”. On the little table in front stand coffee things."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","A rimmed board with round pieces stacked on it."],["The standing onlooker","A man with a sash at his waist stands behind, looking down at the game."],["The old man, left","An older man with a wrapped headcloth, deep in thought."],["The man, right","With a white cloth over his head, he leans over the board."],["The coffee things","A tray and small cups on a fretwork side table."],["The moving hand","The right-hand player’s hand, about to take a piece."]]},"circo":{"title":"Circus Game (Juego del Circo)","artist":"Manuel Manilla (?)","date":"Undated","place":"Mexico","game":"A betting game with two dice","medium":"Relief engraving or photo relief etching on tan wove paper","size":"26.2 × 35.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A game sheet attributed, with a question mark, to the Mexican printmaker Manuel Manilla. At bottom right it names its seller, the print shop “El Teatro”, with an address. Cheap sheets like this were sold in the streets and, it seems, played on just as they were.","A circus tent sits in the middle, ringed by trick riders, a human tower, a strongman and more, each marked with a number from 2 to 12. The “Explanation” at top left says anyone, children too, may play: throw two dice and put a stake on the matching square, or take the stakes already there. Whoever throws 12 takes everything."],"spots":[["The circus tent","The title “JUEGO DEL CIRCO” and the tent, which is square 12."],["The rules","The rules, headed “ESPLICACION” (explanation)."],["The trick rider","A dancer on the back of a galloping horse: square 6."],["The human tower","One man standing on another’s shoulders: square 4."],["The strongman","A man hoisting a cannon-like weight: square 8."],["The print shop’s name","“De venta, Estampería ‘El Teatro’” and the address."]]},"loteria":{"title":"Lotería Picture Cards","artist":"José Guadalupe Posada","date":"1880–1913","place":"Mexico","game":"Lotería (a picture-calling game)","medium":"Relief engraving or photo relief etching on tan wove paper","size":"30.0 × 40.0 cm","credit":"","p":["The Mexican printmaker Posada drew a great many images for the Mexico City publisher Antonio Vanegas Arroyo, whose cheap sheets carried news, songs and games. This one bears the title “JUEGO DE LOTERIA” and Vanegas Arroyo’s name.","Thirty-eight small named pictures fill the sheet: a star, the moon, a scorpion, the devil, a skull, a rooster, a printing shop and more. In lotería, like bingo, a caller draws cards and calls out the pictures, and players mark matching pictures on their boards with beans or the like. The sheet was probably cut up into cards."],"spots":[["Title and publisher","“JUEGO DE LOTERIA” and the name of Vanegas Arroyo’s shop."],["The star","“ESTRELLA”, the first picture at top left."],["The moon","“LUNA”, a crescent with a face."],["Death","“MUERTE”: a skeleton with a scythe, playing a pipe."],["The pulque gatherer","“TLACHIQUERO”: a man collecting agave sap."],["The print shop","“IMPRENTA”: workers at a press, where sheets like this were made."]]},"jeupol":{"title":"“Jeu polonais”, or Draughts Board","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1833–1900","place":"Belgium or the Netherlands","game":"Draughts (10 × 10 board)","medium":"Woodcut with letterpress text","size":"32.2 × 39.6 cm","credit":"","p":["A single sheet titled in French and Dutch, “JEU POLONAIS OF DAMSPEL”. “Jeu polonais” (the Polish game) was the French name for draughts on a 10 × 10 board. At top right the publisher’s serial number reads “n. 106”.","As the museum’s record says, there are 100 squares, 50 white and 50 black, with ornamental borders left and right. No pieces are printed, so it was probably a cheap board played on as it was, with beans or buttons for men. The paper still shows its folds."],"spots":[["The title","The words “JEU POLONAIS OF DAMSPEL”."],["The serial number","The publisher’s number “n. 106”."],["The left border","A column of round flower motifs."],["The right border","The same pattern on the other side."],["The hundred squares","The 10 × 10 board; the dark squares are hatched with fine lines."],["The fold","A fold line left in the paper; perhaps it was carried folded."]]},"dkaart":{"title":"Dominoes and Playing Cards to Cut Out","artist":"Anonymous (Brepols)","date":"1800–1833","place":"Belgium (Turnhout)","game":"Dominoes and cards","medium":"Woodcut coloured in blue, red and yellow; letterpress text","size":"34.3 × 41.9 cm","credit":"","p":["According to the museum’s record, this sheet prints 32 playing cards and 28 dominoes to be cut out. It is number 114 in the list of the publisher Brepols, with the retailer “D. Pouwels, Bergen-op-Zoom” at the foot. The title runs in Dutch and French.","On the left, the 28 dominoes from double blank to double six are stacked in a triangle. On the right are 32 cards, seven to ten plus court cards and aces in each suit: a pack for games such as piquet. Because nobody cut it up, we can see it as it left the press."],"spots":[["The title","The words “DOMINO EN KAARTSPEL”."],["Double blank","The top of the triangle: the domino with no pips."],["Double six","The highest piece, at the right end of the bottom row."],["Ace of spades","Second card down in the left-hand column."],["A court card","A king coloured in red, blue and yellow."],["The retailer","“D. POUWELS, Bergen-op-Zoom” at the bottom."]]},"apen":{"title":"Two Monkeys Playing Trictrac","artist":"Quirin Boel, after David Teniers","date":"1635–1690","place":"Flanders","game":"Trictrac (a relative of backgammon)","medium":"Etching","size":"11.0 × 14.5 cm","credit":"","p":["One of a series, a title print and six plates, in which monkeys act out human pursuits. At lower left it reads “D. Teniers invented and published, with privilege”: Quirin Boel etched the design of the Flemish painter Teniers.","As the museum’s record describes, two monkeys sit on the floor with a trictrac board between them. The one on the left drinks wine from a glass; the one on the right, in a hat with a cock’s feather, reaches for a piece. It probably laughs at people given over to drink and gaming."],"spots":[["The board","A folding trictrac board on the floor, with its points and men."],["The left-hand monkey","A monkey in a coat, seated at the board."],["The wine glass","The glass raised by the left-hand monkey."],["The feathered hat","A cock’s feather in the right-hand monkey’s hat."],["The jug","A jug at the left-hand monkey’s feet."],["The inscription","“D Teniers in et excud cum privilegio”."]]},"dstereo":{"title":"Men Playing Dominoes at a Table (Stereograph)","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1850–1880","place":"Unknown (Dutch inscription)","game":"Dominoes","medium":"Albumen print (stereograph)","size":"8.5 × 17.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A stereograph: two almost identical photographs side by side. Seen through a viewer, a stereoscope, they merge into one scene with depth. At the left edge is written by hand “Het dominospel” (the game of dominoes).","A table has been set out in the sun before a building, and men in shirtsleeves hold their dominoes. Two stand and watch the game; the man on the right wears a white cap. As everyone had to keep still, the scene was probably arranged for the camera."],"spots":[["Tiles on the table","Dominoes held face-down in the players’ hands."],["The man in the white cap","Seated at the right, holding his tiles."],["The man with the neckerchief","Standing, looking down at the game."],["The man on the left","A young man leaning on his elbow over his tiles."],["The inscription","“Het dominospel.” written at the left edge."],["The second image","The right-hand picture, taken from a slightly shifted point: together the two look solid."]]},"gojo":{"title":"Two Women Playing Go","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1870–1891","place":"Japan","game":"Probably go","medium":"Hand-coloured albumen print","size":"9.0 × 13.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A black-and-white photograph coloured by hand with a brush. Pictures like this were made in studios in Yokohama and elsewhere as souvenirs for foreigners. This one was mounted in an album of photographs from a journey to Manila.","Before an alcove-like display and a folding screen, two women sit on either side of a footed board, and the woman on the left is placing a stone. White and black stones lie on the board, so it is probably go, though the museum’s record says “a board game”. On the tray in front are sweets and dishes."],"spots":[["Board and stones","A thick footed board with white and black stones."],["The placing hand","The left-hand woman setting down a stone."],["The woman on the left","She turns to face us; her obi is tied in a large knot."],["The woman on the right","In a striped kimono, her hand by the board."],["Tray and dishes","Sweets and cups laid out in front."],["The flowers","A vase of flowers on a stand behind."]]},"meade":{"title":"Two Men Playing Chess","artist":"Charles Richard Meade and Henry William Mathew Meade","date":"1852","place":"United States","game":"Chess","medium":"Daguerreotype","size":"6.9 × 8.3 cm","credit":"","p":["A daguerreotype from the studio of the Meade brothers, English-born photographers working in America. A daguerreotype forms its image directly on a silver-plated copper sheet, so each is unique and cannot be reprinted; this one sits in a small leather case behind a gilt mat.","Two young men face a chessboard on a little stand; the one on the left has his fingers on a piece. The one on the right, hand to his chest, studies the board. Because they had to hold still for a long time, the move is probably a posed one."],"spots":[["The chessboard","The board and white pieces on a stand draped with flowered cloth."],["The man on the left","His fingers on a piece."],["The man on the right","Looking down at the board."],["The hand on the chest","The right-hand player’s thoughtful hand."],["The table cloth","A small stand covered with flowered fabric."],["The gilt mat","The gold mat and case that protect the plate."]]},"weiqidai":{"title":"Landscape with Daoist Immortals Playing Weiqi","artist":"Attributed to Dai Jin","date":"Ming dynasty, 15th–16th century","place":"China","game":"Weiqi (go)","medium":"Hanging scroll; ink and colours on silk","size":"175.2 × 106.7 cm","credit":"","p":["A large hanging scroll attributed to the Ming painter Dai Jin (c. 1388–1462). From Zhejiang and known for vigorous landscapes, he was later named the founder of the Zhe school. Beneath towering cliffs and a great tree, the people are drawn small.","On the ground in the shade, two immortals sit at a ruled weiqi board, with a dish of fruit and a jar beside them. The figure crouching at left recalls the woodcutter who watched immortals play in the mountains until his axe handle rotted away (the Lanke tale), though the museum’s title speaks only of Daoist immortals playing weiqi."],"spots":[["The board","A rimmed board drawn with a fine grid."],["The bearded immortal","An immortal with a long beard, seated at ease."],["The immortal in the blue cap","Leaning over the board; a bowl of stones lies by his knee."],["The crouching figure","Peering at the board from the left; perhaps the woodcutter of the Lanke tale."],["Fruit and jar","A dish and jar set beyond the board."],["Signature and seals","A signature and two red seals at upper right."]]},"reise":{"title":"The Journey round the World, by Water and by Land","artist":"Anonymous","date":"19th century","place":"Probably German-speaking lands","game":"Race game with one die, played for stakes","medium":"Lithograph","size":"42.5 × 33.7 cm","credit":"","p":["A game sheet titled in German, “The journey round the world, by water and by land: a new and entertaining dice game”. A round board holds spaces 1 to 41, with pictures of a balloon, sailing ships, a steamship, a railway engine, people of various nations and more. It is numbered “No. 107” at bottom right.","Below, the rules are printed in German, English, Dutch and French. Each player first pays 15 marks into the shared “travel purse”, then throws one die in turn and moves a marker. Landing on a large picture wins 2 marks, a number without a picture costs a 2-mark fine, and whoever reaches 41 takes all the rest."],"spots":[["41: the stagecoach","The goal at the centre: a coach with passengers and driver."],["1: the Englishman","The starting space, “Engländer” in a top hat."],["The balloon","A space with a balloon in the sky."],["The sailing ship","A ship under full sail."],["The railway engine","A steam locomotive puffing smoke."],["The rules","The rules, printed in four languages."]]},"huchten":{"title":"Casting the Dice for Life or Death","artist":"Jan van Huchtenburg","date":"1680–1720","place":"The Netherlands","game":"Dice","medium":"Oil on canvas","size":"67.5 × 76.0 cm","credit":"","p":["By the Dutch painter Jan van Huchtenburg (1647–1733), known for battle scenes, and signed at lower left. The museum’s title is “The dice game for life”. Outside a fortress, two prisoners throw dice on a drum, surrounded by officers and soldiers.","A gallows stands at the back right, and soldiers watch from the fortress wall at left. It probably shows condemned soldiers casting lots by dice to decide who will be executed. One prisoner points at the drum; the other kneels to look at the throw."],"spots":[["Drum and dice","A drum serves as the table where the dice are thrown."],["The pointing prisoner","Standing, pointing at the drum."],["The kneeling prisoner","In a white shirt, bending over the drum."],["The mounted officer","In a hat, looking down from his horse."],["The gallows","The gallows rising at upper right."],["Soldiers on the wall","Soldiers watching from the fortress wall."]]},"vertu":{"title":"The Game of Virtue (Spel der Deugd)","artist":"Jan Gerritsz. Visser (designed by J. Voorman)","date":"1798","place":"The Netherlands","game":"Betting game with two dice","medium":"Engraving, etching and letterpress","size":"56.9 × 38.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A large game sheet titled in Dutch and French, “The Game of Virtue”. In a temple of columns sits Virtue at the centre, with Prosperity standing at left and Poverty at right. Medallions of virtues such as Sincerity, Gratitude and Love hang on the columns, and pictures of vices such as Avarice, Pride and Sloth fill the bases below.","According to the rules beneath, it can be played with two dice or by drawing lots. With dice, the players stake 63 in all, then throw in turn following the course of the sun. Each throw names a virtue or vice: double six, Virtue, takes the pot, and double one, Vice, pays eight. Under each picture, “T” is what you take and “B” what you pay."],"spots":[["The title","“SPEL DER DEUGD” in the pediment."],["Virtue","Virtue (LA VERTU) enthroned at the centre; double six takes the pot."],["Prosperity","Prosperity with her horn of plenty."],["Vice","Double one, Vice (LE VICE): a child raising a cup."],["Avarice","Avarice (L’AVARICE) on the base; “B” and the dice show what is paid."],["The rules","The rules, closely printed below."]]},"charros":{"title":"The Charro Smugglers: A Dice Game","artist":"José Guadalupe Posada","date":"Undated","place":"Mexico","game":"Race game with three dice","medium":"Lithograph","size":"29.9 × 40.4 cm","credit":"","p":["A race game with pictures by Posada, titled “LOS CHARROS CONTRABANDISTAS / JUEGO DE DADOS” (The Charro Smugglers / Dice Game). Below runs the publisher’s name, “Imprenta de A. Vanegas Arroyo, México”, and Posada signed at lower right. Charros are Mexican horsemen; in the corners they gallop in sombreros.","Rope-bordered round spaces wind across the board, holding animals, people, a train and more. The “Explanation” at upper right says three dice are used. Three ones on your first throw wins; landing on the skull loses; the deer collects from the pot, the robbers make you pay, and the lion holds you until someone else arrives."],"spots":[["The title","“LOS CHARROS CONTRABANDISTAS / JUEGO DE DADOS”."],["The rules","The rules, headed “EXPLICACION”."],["The players","People round a table, playing at a board, lower left."],["The dice","Three dice drawn in the middle of the board."],["The charro on horseback","A charro galloping and swinging his lasso."],["The publisher","“IMPRENTA DE A. VANEGAS ARROYO, MÉXICO”."]]},"snuffwq":{"title":"Snuff Bottle with Figures Playing Weiqi","artist":"China","date":"Qing dynasty, Daoguang reign mark and period (1820–50)","place":"China","game":"Weiqi (go)","medium":"Porcelain painted in overglaze enamels","size":"Height 5.7 cm","credit":"","p":["A small bottle for carrying powdered tobacco in Qing China. The museum’s record notes a Daoguang reign mark and dates it to 1820–50. On its white porcelain body, about six centimetres tall, a mountain scene and figures are painted in overglaze enamels.","Beside a cave-like rock, two old men sit at a weiqi board on a blue stand; the one on the left reaches to place a stone. Behind him a boy with a staff looks on. The subject probably recalls the tale of the woodcutter who watched immortals at weiqi."],"spots":[["The board","A board on a blue stand, painted with a fine grid."],["The bearded elder","In purple robes, reaching towards the board."],["The elder opposite","Seated at right, raising a hand as he looks."],["The boy with a staff","Watching from behind."],["The grotto","A cave-like rock behind the players."],["The stopper","A green stopper."]]},"ostade":{"title":"Trictrac Players in an Inn","artist":"Adriaen van Ostade","date":"1653","place":"The Netherlands (Haarlem)","game":"Trictrac (a relative of backgammon)","medium":"Etching","size":"8.5 × 7.4 cm","credit":"","p":["A palm-sized etching by the Haarlem painter Ostade, known for scenes of peasant life, signed “A. ostade” below. In a dark inn, light from a leaded window falls on the people.","At the table on the left two men face each other over a trictrac board while a third peers from behind. On the right a man leans back against the wall with a cup in his hand. A big jug lies on the floor; it seems a lazy afternoon in the inn."],"spots":[["The board","A trictrac board on the table."],["The left-hand player","A man in a hat, facing the board."],["The right-hand player","Bent over the board."],["The onlooker","A man standing behind to watch."],["The man at rest","Seated by the wall, cup in hand."],["The leaded window","A window with diamond panes, where the light comes in."]]},"lenain":{"title":"The Dice Players","artist":"Follower of Mathieu Le Nain","date":"1630–1680","place":"France","game":"Dice","medium":"Oil on canvas","size":"92.8 × 119.7 cm","credit":"","p":["A painting by a follower of Mathieu, youngest of the French Le Nain brothers. The museum notes that a sketch of a similar composition by Gabriel de Saint-Aubin is in the Bibliothèque nationale in Paris.","Three men sit round a small round table outdoors, throwing dice; two others look on, the one at left clutching a bottle. Two slender hounds stand at right, and beyond the opening a wide landscape stretches away. The little white things on the table are the dice."],"spots":[["Dice on the table","Dice lying on the round table."],["The man in the pale hat","Seated, reaching out to check the throw."],["The man in black","A young man standing, hand on hip."],["The boy behind","Watching the game from behind the table."],["The man with the bottle","At left, smiling out at us."],["The two hounds","Slender dogs at the right."]]},"tunisie":{"title":"Card Game outside an Arab Café (Tunisian Section, Antwerp World’s Fair)","artist":"Anonymous (printed by Ch. Bernhoeft)","date":"1894","place":"Belgium (Antwerp)","game":"Cards","medium":"Collotype","size":"15.4 × 21.6 cm","credit":"","p":["One of a set of 49 plates of the 1894 Antwerp World’s Fair, printed “EXPOSITION UNIVERSELLE D’ANVERS 1894” above and “Tunisia: card game outside an Arab café” below. It was printed by Bernhoeft, court photographer in Luxembourg.","It was taken in a square with domes and a tower built for the fair’s Tunisian section. Men in Tunisian dress play cards around small tiled tables; one smokes a water pipe, and a tray of cups stands nearby. Visitors in suits sit at right: it was probably a display of life in a distant land."],"spots":[["The card table","Men holding cards around a small tiled table."],["The water pipe","The hose of the water pipe held by the man at left."],["Visitors in suits","Men in suits seated at right, probably fairgoers."],["The tray of cups","Small cups on a tray below the table."],["Domes and tower","Tunisian-style buildings put up for the fair."],["The caption","“TUNISIE” and “PARTIE DE CARTES DEVANT UN CAFÉ ARABE”."]]},"laghouat":{"title":"Caids from around Laghouat","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1880–1890","place":"Algeria (near Laghouat)","game":"Probably dominoes","medium":"Albumen print","size":"20.9 × 27.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A photograph titled “Algerian types: caids from around Laghouat”, mounted in an album and printed with the number “239”. A caid was the title of a local chief in Algeria under French rule.","Men in white robes sit on a mat before a mud wall, laying out long tiles on a low table. From the way they are laid it is probably dominoes, though the museum’s record says only “a game”. In front lie a tea tray and the slippers they have taken off."],"spots":[["Tiles on the low table","Long tiles laid out in a line on the table."],["The tea tray","Glasses and a pot on a tray in front."],["The slippers","Shoes left beside the mat."],["The standing figure","Someone standing by the wall, looking on."],["The mud wall","The rough earthen wall behind."],["The mat","The woven mat the men sit on."]]},"hangong":{"title":"Palace Ladies (Spring Morning in the Han Palace), detail","artist":"Copy after Qiu Ying","date":"Qing dynasty (1644–1911)","place":"China","game":"Weiqi (go)","medium":"Handscroll; ink and colour on silk","size":"36.2 × 454.4 cm (whole scroll)","credit":"","p":["A detail from a handscroll over four and a half metres long, a Qing-period copy after a composition by the Ming painter Qiu Ying (1494–1552). Across a palace on a spring day, court ladies, girls and maids ride a swing, play games, admire peonies, make music, play the qin and feed a parrot and fish.","In this scene two women face each other over a weiqi board behind a balustrade, while others lean in to watch. In front stands a scholar’s rock in a lotus-shaped basin, and maids carry instruments and bundles. As the museum notes, the scroll idealises the life of women in the inner quarters of the palace."],"spots":[["The board","A weiqi board on a table behind the balustrade."],["The player placing a stone","A woman in pale purple reaching to the board."],["The onlookers","Women bending over to see the position."],["The lady with a fan","A woman holding a round fan between the pillars."],["The scholar’s rock","A blue rock set in a lotus-shaped basin."],["The maid with an instrument","A maid carrying what is probably a pipa."]]},"homerbg":{"title":"Backgammon","artist":"Winslow Homer","date":"c. 1877","place":"United States","game":"Backgammon","medium":"Wood engraving","size":"","credit":"","p":["A wood engraving after a drawing by the American painter Winslow Homer (1836–1910), who as a young man drew many illustrations for prints like this. Printed beneath is “396.—BACKGAMMON.—WINSLOW HOMER.”, so it was probably one of a numbered series in a book or magazine.","Two women sit facing each other on an ornate sofa, playing backgammon on a board laid across their laps. A round white object rests on the board, though what it is remains unclear. The folds of the long skirts and the sofa’s scrolled arms are cut in fine lines."],"spots":[["The board","The board laid across the two women’s laps."],["The woman on the left","Hair pinned up, reaching to the board."],["The woman on the right","Leaning forward to study the board."],["The sofa arm","A scroll-shaped arm."],["The skirts","Long skirts, their folds cut in fine lines."],["The caption","“396.—BACKGAMMON.—WINSLOW HOMER.” beneath."]]},"chessst":{"title":"Man and Woman Playing Chess (Stereograph)","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1852–1858","place":"Unknown","game":"Chess","medium":"Stereograph, coloured","size":"8.3 × 16.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A pair of photographs mounted as a stereograph, known in English as “The Chess Players”. Seen through a stereoscope, the board and pieces seem to stand out in depth. Colour has been added over the black-and-white image, on the red curtain and the yellow dress.","The woman in yellow has her hand on a piece, while the man opposite, seen from behind, studies the board. Red and white pieces stand mid-game, but the scene was probably arranged in a studio. Stereographs of such domestic scenes sold widely in the mid-19th century."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","Red and white pieces on the chequered board."],["The moving hand","The woman’s hand on a piece."],["The woman","In a yellow dress with a white collar, looking down at the board."],["The man","Seated with his back to us."],["The red curtain","The coloured curtain behind."],["The second image","The right-hand view; through a stereoscope the pair gains depth."]]},"corrida":{"title":"Bullfight Dice Game (Corrida de Toros)","artist":"José Guadalupe Posada","date":"1898","place":"Mexico","game":"Race game with two dice, played for stakes","medium":"Photo relief etching on wove paper","size":"40.4 × 30.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A race game with pictures by Posada, titled “CORRIDA DE TOROS” (Bullfight). The spaces run in a spiral, showing the passes and moments of a bullfight in order from space 1 on the outside towards the centre. A bullring stands in the middle, and below is the name of Antonio Vanegas Arroyo’s press.","According to the “Explanation” at the foot, two or more players put in stakes and move by throwing two dice. Five, the “pole vault”, goes on to 10; twelve, the “goring of the picador”, pays and misses turns; 28, the “chest pass”, starts over. Whoever reaches 32, “dragging out the bull”, takes all the stakes."],"spots":[["The title","The words “CORRIDA DE TOROS”."],["The bullring","The bullring building at the heart of the spiral."],["Space 1","The start, at the mouth of the spiral."],["The bull’s head","A horned bull’s head at upper right."],["The bullfighter","A torero’s portrait at lower left."],["The rules","The “EXPLICACION” printed along the foot."]]},"ambrocards":{"title":"Three Men Playing Cards","artist":"Unknown maker (American)","date":"1865","place":"United States","game":"Cards","medium":"Ambrotype","size":"14.0 × 11.0 cm","credit":"","p":["An ambrotype is a thin negative on glass, backed with black so that it reads as a positive. Cheaper than the daguerreotype, it was widely used in 1850s–60s America. This one sits behind a gilt mat in a case lined with red velvet.","Before a plain backdrop, three men sit round a small table holding their cards. The man at left wears a bowler and the one at right a cap, both with cigars in their mouths; the man in the centre, in a dark jacket, looks at us. They probably posed as if in the middle of a game for the camera."],"spots":[["The hands","Hands holding cards over the table."],["The man in the bowler","At left, with a cigar."],["The man in the centre","In a dark jacket, looking out at us."],["The man in the cap","At right, with a cigar."],["The gilt mat","The stamped gold mat round the image."],["The case","The red velvet-lined case."]]},"morphy":{"title":"Paul Morphy, The Chess Champion","artist":"Winslow Homer","date":"1859","place":"United States","game":"Chess","medium":"Wood engraving","size":"","credit":"","p":["A portrait wood engraving printed “PAUL MORPHY, THE CHESS CHAMPION”, after a drawing by Winslow Homer (1836–1910), then a young illustrator. Morphy (1837–1884), from New Orleans, toured Europe in 1858–59, defeating one leading master after another, and came home famous.","The young Morphy sits with his left hand resting on the edge of a board, beside a king, a rook, pawns and other pieces. Made in 1859, when he returned home a celebrity, it was probably printed as an illustration in a newspaper or magazine."],"spots":[["The pieces","A king, a rook, pawns and others on the board."],["The hand on the board","His left hand at the board’s edge."],["Morphy’s face","The champion, barely past twenty."],["The bow tie","A neatly dressed collar."],["The hand on the knee","His right hand resting on his knee."],["The title","“PAUL MORPHY, THE CHESS CHAMPION” beneath."]]},"dutton":{"title":"The Dutton Family in the Drawing Room of Sherborne Park, Gloucestershire","artist":"Johann Zoffany","date":"c. 1772","place":"England","game":"Cards","medium":"Oil on canvas","size":"101.5 × 127.0 cm","credit":"","p":["By Johann Zoffany (1733–1810), a German-born painter who made his career in England. The conversation piece, showing a family informally, was invented in eighteenth-century England and became very popular. The setting is the drawing room of Sherborne Park in Gloucestershire.","According to the museum, father and daughter, mother and son are working out their next move in a game of cards. The family had recently lost a loved one, so the parents wear full mourning black, and the children lesser mourning in mauve, white and grey."],"spots":[["The card table","A green-covered table with cards."],["The father","In black, holding his cards."],["The daughter","In white, studying her hand."],["The son","In grey, holding his cards."],["The mother","In black mourning dress."],["The fireplace","A marble fireplace with a lit fire."]]},"whist":{"title":"The X.mas Academics: A Combination Game at Whist","artist":"James Bretherton (probably after H. W. Bunbury)","date":"1773","place":"England","game":"Whist (cards)","medium":"Etching","size":"26.5 × 39.6 cm","credit":"","p":["According to the museum, a satire on the academics of Cambridge. At lower left it seems to read “H. W. Bunbury del. 1772” and at lower right “J. Bretherton f.”; it was published in 1773 from a shop in New Bond Street, London. Academic caps hang on the wall.","Four gentlemen play the card game whist while pulling faces at one another. Whist is played by two pairs of partners, so the “combination game” of the title is probably a pun. Behind them one man makes a mocking sign with his fingers over another’s head. Two servants and two dogs are close by."],"spots":[["Cards on the table","Cards played onto the table."],["The bewigged academic","A scholar in a large wig, hand held close."],["The finger sign","A man behind raising his fingers over another’s head."],["The maid","A servant in a hat, carrying a tray."],["The academic caps","University caps hanging on the wall."],["The dog at right","A small dog barking at lower right."]]},"hey":{"title":"Three Men at Chess","artist":"Paul Hey","date":"1877–1952","place":"Germany","game":"Chess","medium":"Print (probably etching)","size":"19.3 × 26.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A print by the German painter and illustrator Paul Hey (1867–1952). Three elderly men lean in over a single chessboard; fine layered lines draw their deep wrinkles and white beards.","As the museum’s record notes, the man at left rests his hand on his chin, and the man at right holds a cigar as he reaches over the pieces. On the left of the table are two coffee cups, a glass and a box of matches. It is probably a game in a café."],"spots":[["The chessboard","Board and pieces on the table."],["The man with hand to chin","White-bearded, deep in thought."],["The man in the middle","Leaning forward, reaching for a piece."],["The man at right","Peering in from behind."],["The hand with the cigar","A cigar held between the fingers."],["Coffee and matches","Two cups, a glass and a matchbox at left."]]},"artful":{"title":"The Artful Card, or, One Too Many for Granny. Hearts Are Trumps!","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1852–1863","place":"Probably Britain","game":"Cards","medium":"Stereograph, coloured","size":"8.4 × 17.4 cm","credit":"","p":["The English title is “The artful card, or, one too many for granny. Hearts are trumps!” A comic little scene of getting the better of granny with a sly card, made into a pair of stereo photographs. The English title suggests it was probably made in Britain.","At a round table granny and a girl play cards, while the young woman in green, holding her hand of cards, is embraced from behind by a young man. “Hearts are trumps” is probably a pun on the card suit and on love. The room with its spinning wheel and dresser is surely a studio set."],"spots":[["The young woman’s cards","The cards held by the woman in green."],["The young man","His hand on her shoulder from behind."],["Granny","An older woman in a headscarf, eyes on her cards."],["The girl","Smiling as she plays a card."],["The table","A round table with cards and small things on it."],["The dresser","A glass-fronted cupboard of crockery."]]},"cezcards":{"title":"A Study for the Card Players","artist":"Paul Cézanne","date":"1890–92","place":"France","game":"Cards","medium":"Watercolour with graphite on beige wove paper","size":"36.2 × 48.5 cm","credit":"","p":["In the 1890s, at Aix in the south of France, Cézanne painted several pictures of men playing cards. This is a watercolour study for that series: a moustached man in a hat sits at a table, looking at his hand.","Over graphite lines, thin washes of blue and grey capture the folds of his coat and the weight of his body. The right-hand side and the far end of the table are left unfinished, with colour only around the figure. His models are said to have been local working men."],"spots":[["The face","Eyes lowered under the hat, studying his cards."],["The cards","Cards held in both hands."],["The table","A table drawn in pencil lines only."],["The chair","The chair back at left."],["The coat","Folds built up in blue and grey."],["The unfinished part","Near the table legs only the lines remain."]]},"vuillardck":{"title":"The Game of Checkers, from Landscapes and Interiors","artist":"Édouard Vuillard","date":"1899","place":"France","game":"Draughts (checkers)","medium":"Colour lithograph on greyish-ivory China paper","size":"36.9 × 28.5 cm","credit":"","p":["Plate one of Landscapes and Interiors, an album of colour lithographs by the French painter Vuillard, published by the dealer Ambroise Vollard and printed by the lithographic printer Auguste Clot.","In a room thick with patterned wallpaper, a bearded man sits at a draughts board while a woman in a checked dress stands with her hand to her chin, looking on. Figures, wallpaper and sofa melt into flat colour and pattern; only the black-and-white squares of the board stand out sharply."],"spots":[["The board","The black-and-white draughts board."],["The bearded man","Sitting at the board."],["The standing woman","In a checked dress, hand to her chin."],["The wallpaper","Wallpaper filled with swirling lines."],["The flowered cushion","Patterned fabric at lower left."],["The lamp","A slender lamp-like shape against the wall."]]},"cruikchess":{"title":"Game of Chess","artist":"George Cruikshank, after Captain Frederick Marryat","date":"1814","place":"England","game":"Chess","medium":"Hand-coloured etching","size":"23.8 × 34.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A print by the English caricaturist Cruikshank after a sketch by Marryat, naval officer and later novelist, published in London by Hannah Humphrey. The title “GAME of CHESS” runs along the bottom.","At a candlelit table, two elderly gentlemen face a chessboard. Behind each stands an onlooker: the one at left holds a finger to his lips for silence, and the one at right peers at the board through a quizzing glass. It probably pokes fun at kibitzers who cannot keep quiet."],"spots":[["The board","The chessboard with its black and white men."],["The candles","Two candles lighting the board."],["The man saying hush","An onlooker with a finger to his lips."],["The man with the glass","An onlooker peering through a quizzing glass."],["The sleeping dog","A dog asleep under the table."],["The title","“GAME of CHESS” at the foot."]]},"domino98":{"title":"Woman and Child Playing Dominoes at Table","artist":"Artist unknown (American)","date":"c. 1898","place":"United States","game":"Dominoes","medium":"Albumen print affixed to glass","size":"40.8 × 51.1 cm","credit":"","p":["A large photograph, probably made in an American studio, finished by mounting it on glass. A woman in white and a curly-haired girl sit on either side of a small table decorated with flowers.","Dominoes lie on the table, and the woman is setting one down. The girl rests her chin on her hand and watches. The curtains, ornate cabinet and pictures on the wall are probably a studio backdrop."],"spots":[["The dominoes","White dominoes laid out on the table."],["The girl","Chin in hand, watching the tiles."],["The woman","In white, laying down a tile."],["The flowers","A vase of flowers on the table."],["The cabinet","An ornate cabinet at back right."],["The inscription","White lettering at lower right."]]},"langkat":{"title":"Albert Glaser Playing Cards with Two Friends on a Boat, Langkat, Sumatra","artist":"Anonymous (H. Ernst & Co.)","date":"1895–1905","place":"Indonesia (Langkat, Sumatra)","game":"Cards","medium":"Photograph","size":"17.5 × 22.8 cm","credit":"","p":["A photograph taken in Langkat, northern Sumatra. At lower right is the blind stamp of the studio “H. Ernst & Co. / Bindjey Langkat”, and on the back a handwritten note says the man in the middle is Albert Glaser, a lady’s brother.","On the deck of a boat under an awning, three men in white sit at a table with cards in their hands. Cards and small coin-like pieces lie on the table, and a bottle stands by the man at right. The white clothes and the awning speak of life in a hot climate."],"spots":[["On the table","Dealt cards and coin-like pieces."],["Glaser","The man in the middle fanning his cards; the note on the back names him."],["The man on the left","Seen half from behind, cards in hand."],["The man on the right","With a moustache, cards in hand at the table."],["The bottle","A bottle beside the man at right."],["The awning","Leafy trimmings hanging from the awning."]]},"chatur":{"title":"A Game of Chaturanga","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1805–1815","place":"India","game":"Chaturanga","medium":"Gouache and gold, brush","size":"16.2 × 9.8 cm (image)","credit":"","p":["An Indian miniature painting. According to the museum’s record, two women are playing chaturanga, a board game, while a maid holds a fly whisk. Chaturanga is an old Indian board game, regarded as the ancestor of chess.","Beneath white arches, on a red rug, a woman in dark blue and a woman in orange face each other over a chequered board. The woman in orange holds out her hand as if speaking. The picture is framed by a deep red border edged with black and white lines."],"spots":[["The board","A chequered board on the red rug."],["The woman in blue","Adorned with gold, reaching to the board."],["The woman in orange","Facing her with an outstretched hand."],["The maid","Standing behind."],["The fly whisk","Held up by the maid."],["The red border","A deep red frame edged in black and white."]]},"shabbarat":{"title":"Evening in the Women’s Quarters","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1760–1770","place":"India","game":"Chaupar (pachisi)","medium":"Gouache and gold, brush","size":"34.3 × 25.6 cm","credit":"","p":["An Indian miniature. According to the museum’s record, four women are playing a board game in a wide palace courtyard, the game called chaupar or pachisi. They sit round a cross-shaped cloth board.","The woman at front right holds three long dice, ready to throw. Beside her, leaning on a bolster, is the most important lady. It is night, but lamps light the terrace everywhere, and countless lights line the distant city walls. The record suggests the Islamic festival of Shab-i-Barat is being celebrated."],"spots":[["The cross-shaped board","The cross-shaped board spread on the floor."],["The woman with the dice","Holding long dice at the front."],["The lady on the bolster","The highest-ranking lady."],["The maid","Approaching with a tray."],["A lamp","A candle burning under a glass cover."],["The distant pavilion","A garden with a fountain, a pavilion and the city’s lights."]]},"govase":{"title":"Ovoid Vase with Women Playing Go","artist":"Anonymous (seal of ‘Tang Jintang’)","date":"1724","place":"China (Guangdong)","game":"Go (weiqi)","medium":"Porcelain with overglaze enamels and gilding","size":"Height 15.3 cm","credit":"","p":["A Chinese vase of very thin porcelain, painted in enamels. On the back an inscription reads “a picture of a game of go, painted on the day of the Flower Festival in the jiachen year, in a comfortable house on the Pearl River in Lingnan”, with the red seal “Tang Jintang”. It belonged to the porcelain collection of the Hague collector Jean Theodore Royer.","Two women face each other over a go board on a table; the one on the left holds a bowl of stones. At right, two women and two children lean in to watch. On the stand behind are antiques, among them a vase holding peacock feathers and coral."],"spots":[["The board","A go board on the table, with black and white stones."],["The player on the left","Holding a stone bowl and pointing."],["The player on the right","Leaning over the board."],["The onlookers","Women peering at the board from behind."],["Peacock feathers and coral","In a vase on the stand."],["The stone bowl","Held by the woman on the left."]]},"yokihi":{"title":"A Man Playing Backgammon with a Lady (Emperor Xuanzong and Yang Guifei)","artist":"Anonymous (surimono)","date":"1822","place":"Japan","game":"Ban-sugoroku (Japanese backgammon)","medium":"Colour woodblock print with metallic pigments","size":"21.6 × 19.1 cm","credit":"","p":["A surimono, a luxury print combining a picture with poetry, printed on thick paper with metallic powders and commissioned by poets as gifts for friends. This one carries one poem and the poet’s name, Tachibana no Goen.","According to the museum’s record, the Tang emperor Xuanzong (685–762) is playing sugoroku with his favourite consort Yang Guifei (719–756), a game related to backgammon and trictrac. Black and white men stand on a box-shaped board resting on a red stand."],"spots":[["The board","A box-shaped sugoroku board on a red stand, with men."],["Yang Guifei","With flowers in her hair, her hand on the board."],["Xuanzong","Hand to his chin, watching the board."],["The poem","A poem and the poet’s name at upper left."],["The screen","A folding screen painted with mountains behind."],["Signature and seal","A signature and red seal at lower right."]]},"disderi":{"title":"Men in Seafarers’ Costume Playing Cards","artist":"Disdéri & Cie","date":"1850–1890","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Cards","medium":"Albumen print (carte de visite)","size":"8.6 × 5.3 cm","credit":"","p":["A carte-de-visite photograph from an album, printed on the back with the name of Disdéri & Cie, 8 Boulevard des Italiens, Paris. Disdéri was the photographer who popularized the carte de visite, small portraits printed many at a time like calling cards.","Men in old-fashioned dress, with tricorn hats, long coats and white stockings, sit round a patterned table playing cards. The museum’s record calls it seafarers’ costume, but it is probably dress for a play or a masquerade. The man in the middle stands up to peer at the table."],"spots":[["The table","A table with a patterned border; cards and cups on it."],["The man standing","In a tricorn, he leans over the table."],["The man at left","Seated, cards in hand."],["The man at right","In a tricorn, cards in hand."],["Buckled shoes","White stockings and buckled shoes."],["The gilt frame","The gold border of the mount."]]},"gans":{"title":"Game of the Goose (74-space spiral)","artist":"Jan Oortman (Sr.), after a design by Daniel Willem Stoopendaal","date":"1763–1823","place":"The Netherlands","game":"Game of the goose (race game)","medium":"Woodcut coloured in blue, red and green","size":"40.8 × 46.8 cm","credit":"","p":["A spiral race board printed in the Netherlands. According to the museum, its spaces run from 1 to 74, some with pictures. Jan Oortman cut the block after a design by Stoopendaal, whose name appears at lower right with “invenit”.","The goal, 74, is a temple by the sea at sunset in the centre. Spaces bear words such as “LEER GELD” (tuition fee), “DWAALWEG” (wrong way) and “DOMHEID / WYSHEID” (folly / wisdom), and a skeleton with a scythe marked “EINDE” (the end); it was probably a moral game about the path of life."],"spots":[["74: the temple","The goal: a temple on the shore at sunset."],["1: the start","The first space, at lower left."],["Tuition fee","A “LEER GELD” space, probably where you pay."],["The wrong way","A woodland path marked “DWAALWEG”."],["The end","A skeleton with a scythe: “EINDE”."],["The dice","A space showing a pair of dice."]]},"merelle":{"title":"Merelle and the Kite","artist":"Claudine Stella, after Jacques Stella","date":"1657","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Merelle (nine men’s morris)","medium":"Engraving","size":"11.7 × 14.1 cm","credit":"","p":["Plate 17 of a series of children’s games, engraved by Claudine Stella after designs by her uncle, the painter Jacques Stella. According to the museum it comes from an album of 51 engravings. The players are plump little naked children, or putti.","At front left they play merelle (nine men’s morris) on a board scratched into a stone block. On the ground at right are the lines of franc du carreau, a game of tossing coins onto squares, and further off one child runs with a kite. The verse below says each takes pains to play well."],"spots":[["The merelle board","A board cut into a stone block, with pieces on it."],["The kite","A kite high in the air."],["The kite-flyer","A child running with the string."],["Franc du carreau","Squares and diagonals drawn on the ground."],["The pointing child","Holding something, pointing at the lines."],["The title","“LA MERELLE, & LE CERF VOLANT”."]]},"feldcards":{"title":"Card Party at an Outdoor Table (Stereograph)","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1850–1880","place":"Unknown (stamp of a Groningen firm on the back)","game":"Cards","medium":"Albumen print (stereograph)","size":"8.5 × 17.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A stereograph of a family or group of friends playing cards at a table in a garden, with ivy on a trellis behind. On the back is the stamp of Feldbrugge & Co., Groningen, in the Netherlands, probably the shop or publisher that sold it.","The bearded man at left raises a card high, about to slap it down. A man resting his head on his hand, a boy holding his cards, a white-haired old man and a woman watching from behind complete the moment. Since everyone had to hold still for long, the scene was probably posed."],"spots":[["The raised card","The card held high by the bearded man."],["On the table","Cards already played."],["The boy’s cards","The cards held by the boy seen from behind."],["The old man","The white-haired man seated at right."],["The watching woman","Standing behind, following the game."],["The embroidered chair","A chair in front upholstered in flowered fabric."]]},"tempel":{"title":"Game Board ‘The Temple of Fortune’","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1816","place":"The Netherlands","game":"Race game played with dice","medium":"Woodcut with letterpress text","size":"43.0 × 48.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A game sheet with spaces 1 to 74 winding in a spiral. In the middle stands a round temple by the sea; the rule sheet kept in the same collection calls it “The Temple of Fortune” and describes a throwing game, that is, a dice game.","Most spaces are empty circles, but here and there are roses, thorns, dice, an iron gate, a ferry and other pictures, and words such as “LEER GELD” (tuition fee), “DOMHEID” (folly) and “WYSHEID” (wisdom). It is probably a moral race game, passing good and bad conduct on the way to the temple."],"spots":[["The Temple of Fortune","The seaside temple at the centre: space 74, the goal."],["Space 1","The start, at lower left."],["Tuition fee","A space marked “LEER GELD”."],["The dice","Two dice pictured in the top row."],["The ferry","The “OVER HAAL” (ferry crossing) picture in the bottom row."],["Folly and wisdom","The words “DOMHEID” and “WYSHEID” at right."]]},"verkeer":{"title":"Miniature Verkeer Board","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1690–1710","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Verkeer (a relative of backgammon), chess, nine men’s morris","medium":"Walnut inlaid with olive wood, ebony, boxwood and bone; copper fittings","size":"Closed 5.8 × 5.8 cm (open 11.6 cm)","credit":"","p":["A games board barely six centimetres square that opens like a box. Outside are a chessboard and a nine men’s morris board; inside is a board for verkeer, a Dutch relative of backgammon. It closes with copper hinges and a tiny hook.","Inside are still one die, nine bone pieces, four ebony pieces and three little coins. Its museum number suggests it belongs with the furnishings of Petronella Oortman’s dolls’ house. Small as it is, the wood inlay is careful and built just like a full-size board."],"spots":[["The die","A die no bigger than a grain of rice."],["The bone pieces","Round white pieces of bone."],["The ebony pieces","Black pieces of ebony."],["The points","Triangles inlaid in light and dark wood."],["The hinges","Copper hinges for folding."],["The hook","A tiny copper hook to close it."]]},"silverbd":{"title":"Miniature Silver Games Board with Pieces","artist":"Frederik van Strant II","date":"1739","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Draughts, nine men’s morris","medium":"Silver","size":"5.4 × 5.4 cm","credit":"","p":["A silver board just 5.4 centimetres square: a 64-square board with a raised rim and a ring for hanging. The dark squares are engraved with fine lines, and the other side is engraved with lines for nine men’s morris.","It is struck with the Amsterdam town mark, the date letter E for 1739, and the mark of the silversmith Frederik van Strant II. Fifteen draughtsmen survive with it, eight decorated with a flower and seven with a circle. It was probably a silver miniature for a dolls’ house or a display cabinet."],"spots":[["Flower pieces","Pieces stamped with a flower."],["Circle pieces","Pieces marked with a circle."],["The ring","A ring at the corner of the board."],["Engraved squares","Dark squares engraved with fine lines."],["Plain squares","Smooth, polished light squares."],["The rim","The raised edge round the board."]]},"schaak1550":{"title":"Chess Set with Mounted Pieces","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1550","place":"Germany","game":"Chess","medium":"Boxwood and softwood","size":"Tallest piece 10.5 cm","credit":"","p":["Each piece stands on a turned base ringed with a railing of fleurons, carved with riders and horses. The kings and queens ride horses or mules, as if setting out on a hunt.","According to the museum’s record, the black king rides a rearing horse that tramples a dragon and wears a chain recalling the Order of the Golden Fleece, perhaps marking him as Charles V. A queen rides side-saddle on a mule with a falcon on her hand. At far right a playful piece shows a monkey riding on a man’s back."],"spots":[["The king","A crowned, bearded king on horseback."],["The queen","A queen riding side-saddle on a mule, a falcon on her hand."],["The monkey piece","A monkey riding on a man’s back."],["A comic piece","At far left, one man riding on another."],["Creatures underfoot","Dragons and beasts crouching under the hooves."],["The bases","Turned bases ringed with fleurons."]]},"kraaij":{"title":"Men and Women Playing a Board Game in a Living Room","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1900–1910","place":"Probably the Netherlands","game":"Backgammon","medium":"Printing-out collodion print","size":"8.0 × 11.1 cm","credit":"","p":["A page from a family album that also holds photographs of the wine merchants Kraaij & Co., Bordeaux–Amsterdam. In a living room hung with a chandelier, men and women, perhaps family and friends, sit round a table.","On the table lies an open folding box board, and the women on either side each hold a dice cup. The museum’s record says “a board game”, but from the box board and cups it is probably backgammon. Coffee cups stand nearby and a birdcage behind, giving the feel of a relaxed evening at home."],"spots":[["The box board","A folding box opened out as a board."],["The left dice cup","The cup held by the woman in glasses."],["The right dice cup","The woman in the spotted dress about to throw."],["The birdcage","A birdcage on a stand behind."],["The chandelier","Lamps with glass shades hanging from the ceiling."],["The standing man","At right, looking down at the game."]]},"braekel":{"title":"An Old Man and Woman Playing Draughts","artist":"Ferdinand De Braekeleer (I)","date":"1862","place":"Belgium (Antwerp)","game":"Draughts (checkers)","medium":"Etching","size":"21.8 × 16.5 cm","credit":"","p":["An etching by the Antwerp painter De Braekeleer (1792–1883), signed at lower left “Ferdinand De Braekeleer Antwerpen 1862”. In an old kitchen with a hearth, an old man and woman sit across a small table.","Round pieces stand on the board; the man picks one up while the woman spreads her hand as if answering back. A hat lies on the floor, and a few pieces have fallen there too. Plates line the shelf, and houses can be seen through the window."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","The board and round pieces on the table."],["The old man","Bent forward, picking up a piece."],["The old woman","In a hood, gesturing as she speaks."],["The hat on the floor","The man’s hat set down on the floor."],["The signature","“Ferdinand De Braekeleer Antwerpen 1862”."],["The plates","Large plates on the shelf above the hearth."]]},"billiard":{"title":"Billiards","artist":"After H. W. Bunbury (anonymous printmaker)","date":"1780","place":"England (London)","game":"Billiards","medium":"Etching and stipple","size":"30.0 × 39.1 cm","credit":"","p":["A print after the English caricaturist Henry William Bunbury, published in London in 1780 by Watson & Dickinson. According to the museum it is number four in a series.","Two men play billiards while the others watch, holding their breath. One leans across the table to make his shot; another carries his cue over his shoulder. A cue rack and two round scoring dials hang on the wall. The museum notes that the table seems to be triangular."],"spots":[["Table and balls","The cloth-covered table and its balls."],["The man taking aim","Leaning across the table to play."],["The man with the cue","Waiting with his long cue."],["The scoring dials","Two round dials on the wall, probably for keeping score."],["The cue rack","A row of cues against the wall."],["The laughing pair","Two onlookers at right, laughing together."]]},"baizi":{"title":"One Hundred Children (detail)","artist":"Xu Yanghong","date":"18th century","place":"China","game":"Weiqi (go)","medium":"Handscroll; ink (and light colour) on paper","size":"29.8 × 526 cm (whole scroll)","credit":"","p":["A go scene cut from a handscroll over five metres long by the Chinese professional painter Xu Yanghong. In a garden a hundred children play, make music, read and write. ‘One hundred children’ is an important theme in Asian art, symbolising fertility and a flourishing family.","The scroll is also known for a later copy by the Japanese master Tani Bunchō, now in the same museum. Beside banana leaves and a scholar’s rock, children gather round a go board on a low stand; two play with stone bowls in hand while the others peer in."],"spots":[["The board","A go board on a low stand, with stones."],["The child placing a stone","In a blue jacket, a hand in the stone bowl."],["The other player","In white, leaning over the board."],["Hands behind the head","A child watching from behind."],["The scholar’s rock","A great rock full of holes."],["The feather fan","A child at left holding a feather fan."]]},"schenkcards":{"title":"Two Chinese Women Playing Cards","artist":"Pieter Schenk (II)","date":"1727–1775","place":"The Netherlands (subject: China)","game":"Cards","medium":"Etching","size":"16.5 × 26.2 cm","credit":"","p":["A plate from the first part of “Newly Invented Chinese Figures”, an album of Chinese-style figures and landscapes published by the Amsterdam engraver and publisher Pieter Schenk the Younger. It is chinoiserie, the taste for imagined China that was fashionable in Europe.","According to the museum’s record, two Chinese women play cards in a garden while a woman approaches from the right with a laden tray. The cards on the table look like European playing cards. On the left a rabbit rests on a stand, and a sailing boat lies on the river."],"spots":[["Cards on the table","Cards spread on the table; they look European."],["The woman on the left","Cards in hand, looking at her partner."],["The woman on the right","Laying a card on the table."],["The woman with a tray","Bringing a tray heaped with food."],["The rabbit","Resting on a stand at left."],["The sailing boat","A boat on the river."]]},"gosaucer":{"title":"Saucer with Figures Playing Go","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1725–1735","place":"China","game":"Go (weiqi)","medium":"Porcelain with overglaze enamels and gilding","size":"Diameter 16.4 cm","credit":"","p":["A Chinese saucer painted in the soft overglaze colours called famille rose. On the underside is a mark of a brush, a ruyi sceptre and an ink stick in a double circle. The museum’s records describe the figures both as a man and a woman playing go and as three women, two of them playing.","A go board sits on a table in a room; two players face each other placing stones, while a third stands behind to watch. On the table are yellow stone bowls; behind is a stand with a vase of flowers, and at right a large blue jar."],"spots":[["The board","A go board with stones on the table."],["The player on the left","In pale purple, leaning on the table."],["The player on the right","In pink, placing a stone."],["The onlooker","Standing behind to watch."],["The vase","A flower vase on a stand."],["The blue jar","A large jar at right."]]},"cheetham":{"title":"A Little Card-Party (in Russia)","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1903–1904","place":"Russia","game":"Cards","medium":"Gelatin silver printing-out print","size":"9.2 × 11.5 cm","credit":"","p":["From the album of the English Cheetham family, who lived in Russia; the mount is inscribed in English “a little card-party”. The museum notes that the players are probably English friends visiting Joseph Cheetham.","In a wallpapered room, three people sit at a table with a flowered cloth, their cards fanned out. Two candlesticks stand on the table, and the woman in the middle laughs as she spreads her hand. It was probably a relaxed moment with friends from home in a distant land."],"spots":[["The left man’s cards","The bearded man’s hand of cards."],["The woman’s cards","The woman in the middle fans her cards."],["The man on the right","Leaning in, cards in hand."],["The candlesticks","Two candlesticks on the table."],["The tablecloth","A large cloth with flowers and patterns."],["The inscription","“a little card-party” on the mount."]]},"problem":{"title":"The Problem (Stereograph)","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1852–1863","place":"Unknown","game":"Chess","medium":"Stereograph, coloured","size":"8.4 × 17.4 cm","credit":"","p":["The English title is “The Problem”. Two figures in white wigs and 18th-century-style dress play chess at an ornate table, made into a coloured pair of stereo photographs. “Simons” is written by hand on the back.","The younger one holds both cheeks and stares at the board, while the older man opposite grins. It is probably a staged, theatrical scene in which you can see at a glance who has the problem. A dog lies at their feet."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","The board and pieces on the ornate table."],["The puzzled player","Head in both hands, staring at the board."],["The grinning man","The older man in a blue coat, smiling."],["The dog","A dog lying at their feet."],["The red curtain","The coloured curtain behind."],["The second image","The right-hand view; through a stereoscope it gains depth."]]},"tarocco":{"title":"Ten Cards from a Tarot Pack","artist":"Anonymous (probably after Giuseppe Maria Mitelli)","date":"1660–1718","place":"Italy (Bologna)","game":"Tarocchi (tarot card games)","medium":"Etching and engraving, partly hand-coloured in light brown","size":"Ten cards on a mount","credit":"","p":["Ten cards from a Bolognese tarot pack, pasted side by side on a mount. Tarot was first used not for fortune-telling but for card games with trumps. At top left is a title card; the museum suggests its portrait may be of the artist Mitelli.","The top row shows four figures, among them a crowned man and a rider; the bottom row shows the six to ten of the suit of coins, each with that many coins. The printed lines are partly washed in light brown."],"spots":[["The title card","Top left: a framed portrait and an inscription."],["The crowned man","A crowned figure holding a sceptre."],["The standing woman","A crowned woman holding a cup."],["The rider","A card with a figure on horseback."],["Ten of coins","Bottom left: ten coins."],["Six of coins","Bottom right: six coins."]]},"kindtegel":{"title":"Tiles with Scenes of Children Playing","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1680–1730","place":"The Netherlands","game":"Children’s games (stilts, swing and more)","medium":"Tin-glazed earthenware (Delftware tiles), painted in blue","size":"107 × 80 cm (48 tiles)","credit":"","p":["A field of forty-eight tiles, eight high and six wide. Each tile shows two children at play, painted small in blue, with a little ornament called a “spider” in each corner.","One child walks on stilts, another is pushed on a swing, others crouch over something on the ground: every tile shows a different game. Such “children’s games” tiles were popular in the Netherlands and were probably set into the walls of kitchens and around fireplaces."],"spots":[["Stilts","A child walking on stilts."],["The swing","A child being pushed on a swing."],["A ground game","Children crouching to play with something on the ground."],["Spider corners","The small ornaments in each corner."],["Forty-eight tiles","Eight rows of six tiles."],["The last tile","The tile at the lower right corner."]]},"dammolen":{"title":"Miniature Board for Draughts and Mill","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1740–1760","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Draughts, nine men’s morris (mill)","medium":"Walnut with light filling paste","size":"4.2 × 4.0 × 1.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A tiny walnut board about four centimetres across. Almost square, with a raised rim, it can be used on one side as a draughts board and on the other as a board for mill (nine men’s morris).","On the draughts side, squares are cut out and filled with light paste to make a chequer against the bare wood; on the mill side the grooved lines are filled with the same paste. So small, it was probably made for a dolls’ house."],"spots":[["Filled squares","Squares cut out and filled with light paste."],["Bare wood squares","Squares left in plain walnut."],["The rim","The raised rim round the board."],["The near corner","The corner nearest us."],["The thickness","The side of the board, 1.5 cm high."],["The far row","The row of squares at the back."]]},"karelpiece":{"title":"Draughtsman with a Portrait of Charlemagne (half)","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1525–1575","place":"Germany (Nuremberg)","game":"Draughts or tables","medium":"Boxwood","size":"Diameter 5.3 cm","credit":"","p":["One face of a boxwood draughtsman. In the centre is a profile framed by a laurel wreath, and round it runs the inscription “CAROLVS. MAGNVS. IMPER. AVGVST.” (Charlemagne, emperor).","The museum holds more than a dozen pieces of the same make, each carved with an emperor or empress from ancient Rome to the Middle Ages: Tiberius, Agrippina, Louis the Pious, Otto and others. It was probably a luxurious set of pieces made as a gallery of rulers."],"spots":[["The profile","Charlemagne’s profile within a laurel wreath."],["The wreath","The laurel ring round the portrait."],["CAROLVS","The words “CAROLVS. MAGNVS” above."],["AVGVST","The words “IMPER. AVGVST” at left."],["The star","A small star dividing the inscription."],["The rim","The stepped edge of the piece."]]},"vdberge":{"title":"The Dutch Game, the Good Life! (Card Playing)","artist":"Pieter van den Berge (design and publication)","date":"1694–1737","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Cards","medium":"Etching printed à la poupée in red, blue and green","size":"27.3 × 17.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A satire by the Amsterdam printmaker and publisher Van den Berge, titled “LE JEU HOLLANDOIS. LA BELLE VIE!”. According to the museum, men play cards in a tavern while devils, beside and beneath the table of a sighing man holding up his glass, win his money.","In the Dutch verse below the careless rake laments that dog fights and cockfights, cards and dice have melted his money. On either side two devils tempt him in German to be merry at dice and cards. The picture on the back wall shows dog fights and cockfights too."],"spots":[["The man with the glass","The rake in a great wig, raising his glass."],["The hand of cards","Cards fanned in the man’s hand."],["The devil at left","A devil grinning behind him."],["The devil under the table","Raking up coins spilled on the floor."],["The fight picture","A painting of dog fights and cockfights."],["Title and verse","“LE JEU HOLLANDOIS” and the rake’s lament."]]},"crespi":{"title":"Children Playing Dice","artist":"Giuseppe Maria Crespi","date":"1675–1747","place":"Italy","game":"Dice","medium":"Etching","size":"22.5 × 15.1 cm","credit":"","p":["An etching by the Bolognese painter Crespi, made with his own hand. According to the museum, four children are playing with dice in the street, and a three-line Italian verse is added below.","A crouching child has just rolled the dice on the ground while three others look on. The verse speaks of bones (dice) and numbers, a brief game, and money dwindling; it probably adds a warning against gambling even to a children’s game."],"spots":[["The dice","Small dice lying on the ground."],["The crouching child","Having just thrown the dice."],["The standing child","Peering down at the throw."],["The child at right","Seated on the ground, watching."],["The child behind","Looking out from behind the others."],["The verse","Three lines of Italian verse."]]},"epinal":{"title":"The Great Game of the Goose (Épinal)","artist":"Pellerin & Cie (Épinal)","date":"1791–1900","place":"France (Épinal)","game":"Game of the goose (race game)","medium":"Woodcut coloured in yellow, red, green and blue; letterpress text","size":"39.8 × 59.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A colour sheet from the Pellerin print works at Épinal in eastern France, headed in French “GRAND JEU DE L’OIE”. According to the museum, a spiral of 63 spaces shows geese, other animals and figures, with the Dutch title “GANZENBORD!” and the rules printed in the middle.","By the rules, you throw two dice, and landing on a goose lets you move the same again. The bridge at 6 sends you to 12, the inn at 19 costs two turns, at the well (31) you wait until someone else arrives, the maze (42) sends you back to 30 and death (58) back to the start. You must reach 63 exactly to win. Counters to cut out run down both sides."],"spots":[["63: the goose","The goal, 63: a goose on a pond."],["The rules","The Dutch rules printed in the centre."],["Counters to cut out","Round numbered counters on either side."],["The title","“GRAND JEU DE L’OIE” at the top."],["Children at play","Children playing in the lower left corner."],["The publisher","“PELLERIN & Cie” at upper left."]]},"matham":{"title":"Couple Playing Tric-trac","artist":"Jacob Matham (publisher), verses by Simon Sovius","date":"1621","place":"The Netherlands (Haarlem)","game":"Tric-trac (a relative of backgammon)","medium":"Engraving","size":"17.9 × 20.0 cm","credit":"","p":["The third print in a series of four published by the Haarlem engraver and publisher Matham. According to the museum, an elegantly dressed couple play tric-trac for money at a small table; the purse lies on the table and the woman holds the dice.","The board’s long points and light and dark men are sharply engraved. Below are Latin and Dutch verses; the Dutch warns that not only excess of drink stirs unchaste thoughts, but play and dicing go against all profit. Behind them a boy raises a glass of wine."],"spots":[["The board","A tric-trac board with its points and men."],["The man moving","The man in the hat reaching over the board."],["The woman with the dice","In a great ruff, holding the dice."],["The boy with the glass","Raising a glass of wine behind them."],["The purse","The purse of stakes on the table."],["The verses","Latin and Dutch lines of warning."]]},"jardin":{"title":"Three Men Playing Cards at a Garden Table, with an Onlooker (France)","artist":"Anonymous (a French amateur photographer)","date":"1900–1910","place":"France","game":"Cards","medium":"Albumen print","size":"11.8 × 17.2 cm","credit":"","p":["From the album of a French amateur photographer, which also holds pictures of a family, the Delizy & Doistau distillery, the army and sights around France.","In the shade of a gravelled garden, three men play cards at a small table while an older man with a white beard stands behind and watches. Cards lie on the table, and the man in the straw boater studies his hand. It was probably a relaxed moment among family or friends."],"spots":[["Cards on the table","Cards played onto the table."],["The onlooker","The older man with a white beard, watching from behind."],["The man in the boater","In the middle, studying his hand."],["The man on the right","In a dark coat, cards in hand."],["The man on the left","In a light jacket."],["The garden","The gravel and shrubs of the garden."]]},"lamplight":{"title":"People Playing Cards (under the Lamp)","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1900–1910","place":"Unknown","game":"Cards","medium":"Gelatin silver print","size":"7.5 × 10.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A small photograph from an album, titled by the museum “People playing cards”. Neither photographer nor place is known.","Under the light of an oil lamp hanging from the ceiling, a woman in a striped dress, a girl and some boys sit at a table with a fringed cloth, fanning out their cards. Photographing in a dark room would have been hard; it was probably an attempt to capture a family evening together."],"spots":[["The fringed tablecloth","A patterned cloth with a fringe."],["The lamp","An oil lamp hanging from the ceiling."],["The woman in stripes","Seated in a white striped dress."],["The woman’s cards","The cards she holds like a fan."],["The girl’s cards","The girl in the middle spreads her hand."],["The boy at right","Leaning in, cards in hand."]]},"borduur":{"title":"Embroidered Card-Table Top with a Card Party","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1700–1725","place":"Unknown","game":"Cards","medium":"Gros point and petit point in coloured wool and silk","size":"100 × 185 cm","credit":"","p":["Embroidery from the top of a folding card table, worked in many-coloured wool and silk, with coarser gros point and finer petit point. Inside a shape with canted corners runs a frame of scrolls and flowers.","In the landscape at the centre, two ladies and a gentleman sit round a table playing cards. According to the museum’s record, a purse, cards and other gaming things lie on the table. A table for card games is fittingly decorated with a card game."],"spots":[["The tabletop","The green table with purse and cards."],["The gentleman","A man in a hat, seated at left."],["The lady at centre","A lady seated behind the table."],["The lady at right","A lady seated in blue."],["The trees","Trees growing behind the figures."],["The frame","A frame of scrolls and flowers."]]},"piquet":{"title":"Cloth Printed with a Piquet Game","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1725–1750","place":"Unknown","game":"Piquet (a card game)","medium":"Linen, printed in red and purple","size":"73.0 × 82.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A linen cloth for a card table, printed all over in red and purple with the things of a card game. The museum’s record also describes it as a handkerchief.","Cards for piquet, played with 32 cards from seven to ace, are scattered round the border and across the centre, with a purse in the middle and counters around it. The court cards are of the Paris pattern, with names such as Caesar, Argine and Hogier."],"spots":[["The purse","The stake purse at the centre."],["The court cards","Kings and queens of the Paris pattern."],["The counters","Round counters."],["The border of cards","A band of cards round the cloth."],["Face-down packs","Packs of cards showing their backs."],["The sealed packet","A sealed packet of cards at lower right."]]},"schaakglas":{"title":"Goblet Engraved with Two Men Playing Chess","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1815–1830","place":"Unknown","game":"Chess","medium":"Clear colourless glass, wheel-engraved","size":"Height 13.7 cm","credit":"","p":["A footed clear glass, its picture and words cut with a small spinning wheel. At a little table with two candles, two men face each other across a chessboard.","The man at left, in spectacles, smokes a pipe; the one at right leans over the board, and a wine glass stands on the table. Round it runs a French inscription: knowing the moves is a simple thing; knowing the game is the fruit of genius."],"spots":[["The chessboard","The board with its pieces."],["The man with the pipe","At left, in spectacles, smoking a pipe."],["The leaning man","At right, peering at the board."],["The candles","Two candles beyond the board."],["The wine glass","A glass on the table."],["The inscription","Words engraved in French."]]},"arlequin":{"title":"Harlequin’s Lotto Game","artist":"Anonymous (published by Mindermann & Co.)","date":"1818–1845","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Lotto / race game","medium":"Engraving, hand-coloured, pasted on card","size":"Unknown","credit":"","p":["A game sheet in which the board is the figure of Harlequin himself, standing with legs apart. Square spaces numbered 1 to 32 run over his green costume and between his legs. Below are the title “Arlequin’s Lotto-Spel” and the name of Mindermann & Co. of Amsterdam.","He wears a net mask over his face and holds a hat and what looks like a wooden sword. Some spaces are marked “HW” or “P”. The hand-coloured print was pasted on card, probably to serve as a playing board."],"spots":[["The net mask","The net covering his face."],["Space 1","The start, under his left leg."],["An HW space","A space marked “HW”."],["P 32","Space 32, marked “P”."],["The title","“Arlequin’s Lotto-Spel” at the foot."],["The hat","The hat in his right hand."]]},"vicaire":{"title":"The Curate’s Game","artist":"René Gayant (according to the plate)","date":"1888–1893","place":"France","game":"Cards","medium":"Collotype (according to the museum)","size":"10.4 × 15.6 cm","credit":"","p":["A plate from the Bulletin du Photo-Club de Paris, titled “La partie du Vicaire” (the curate’s game). The margin is printed “Phototype René Gayant” and “Héliogravure Lemercier”, so the photograph is probably by Gayant and the printing by Lemercier. It was bound sideways into the book.","In a kitchen hung with pots and pans, a priest in a black cassock sits at the table studying his cards, while the man opposite also holds a hand. A man in a cap rests his chin on his hand, and a young person, hand to ear, watches the game. The painterly light and composition suit a photo club devoted to art photography."],"spots":[["The priest’s cards","The cards the priest studies."],["The priest","In a black cassock with a white collar."],["The man with cards","Curly-haired, looking down at his hand."],["The young onlooker","Hand to cheek, watching the game."],["The man in the cap","Chin resting on his hand."],["The kitchen wall","Pots and ladles hanging on the wall."]]},"israels":{"title":"The Chess Players","artist":"Isaac Israels","date":"1875–1922","place":"The Netherlands","game":"Chess","medium":"Pastel","size":"40.0 × 57.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A pastel by the Dutch painter Isaac Israels (1865–1934), known for quick, lively scenes of city life in Amsterdam. In a room with a bright window, men gather round a chessboard.","The two in front rest their cheek or chin on a hand, staring at the board, while a man in a cap leans in behind them. The pieces are not drawn in detail but set down as dabs of colour; a long spell of thought seems to pass."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","The chessboard, its pieces dabs of colour."],["The left-hand player","Cheek in hand, gazing at the board."],["The right-hand player","Chin in hand, thinking."],["The man in the cap","Leaning in from behind."],["The window","The bright window at left."],["The chair","A chair at the right."]]},"louisxi":{"title":"Louis XI and His Daughter Anne of Beaujeu","artist":"Adrien Joseph Verhoeven-Ball","date":"1866","place":"Belgium (Antwerp)","game":"Chess","medium":"Print (probably etching)","size":"26.4 × 31.3 cm","credit":"","p":["The Antwerp painter Verhoeven-Ball both designed and made this print (“pinx et sculp”, below). The museum’s title is “Louis XI and his daughter Anne of Beaujeu playing chess”. A line below mentions the second exhibition of the Association of Antwerp Artists.","Louis XI, king of France in the fifteenth century, sits in a fur robe at the table, while his daughter Anne rests her chin on her hand and studies the board. Pieces and a bowl of fruit stand on the table, a dog lies on the floor and an armed guard stands behind. It is probably an imagined scene of the historical father and daughter."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","The chessboard and pieces on the table."],["Louis XI","The king in his hat and fur robe."],["Anne","His daughter, chin in hand, watching the board."],["The sleeping dog","A slender dog stretched on the floor."],["The guard","An armed soldier in the shadows behind."],["The title","“LOUIS XI ET SA FILLE ANNE DE BEAU-JEU”."]]},"bruining":{"title":"Monks Playing Draughts","artist":"Tieleman Cato Bruining","date":"1750–1850","place":"The Netherlands","game":"Draughts (checkers)","medium":"Etching","size":"20.6 × 16.8 cm","credit":"","p":["An etching by the Dutch printmaker Bruining, inscribed at lower left “T. Bruining inv. et fecit aqua fort” (designed and etched). According to the museum, two monks sit at a table playing draughts.","Behind them stand a third monk and a devil dressed in a monk’s habit. Beside the table stands Death, also disguised as a monk, his scythe hidden behind his back. The monks are so absorbed in the game that they do not notice the intruders."],"spots":[["The board","The draughtboard and pieces on the table."],["Death","Death in a monk’s habit, scythe hidden behind him."],["The pair behind","A third monk and the devil in a habit."],["The left-hand player","A monk bent over the board."],["The right-hand player","A monk thinking, arms on the table."],["The signature","“T. Bruining inv. et fecit aqua fort”."]]},"vanvliet":{"title":"Tric-trac Players by Candlelight","artist":"Jan Gillisz. van Vliet","date":"1631–1634","place":"The Netherlands (Leiden)","game":"Tric-trac (a relative of backgammon)","medium":"Etching and engraving","size":"15.2 × 13.4 cm","credit":"","p":["A print by Van Vliet, who worked in Leiden alongside Rembrandt. According to the museum, two figures play tric-trac in a dark room with a canopy bed at left, while a man and a woman stand at right.","A man in black bends low over the board, his back to us, while his opponent laughs with a hand on the board’s edge. The points and men are clearly visible. The light source is hidden, yet only the board and the faces are lit; it is probably a game at night."],"spots":[["The board","A tric-trac board with its points and men."],["The laughing player","Laughing, a hand on the board’s edge."],["The man bending over","In black, back turned, stooping over the board."],["The man in the hat","Standing behind in a broad-brimmed hat."],["The figure in a ruff","At right, a hand raised."],["The bed curtains","Curtains of the canopy bed at left."]]},"hermans":{"title":"Two Women and a Girl at a Chessboard","artist":"François Hermans (studio)","date":"1857–1895","place":"The Netherlands (Rotterdam)","game":"Chess","medium":"Albumen print (carte de visite)","size":"10.1 × 6.2 cm","credit":"","p":["A carte-de-visite photograph from a family album, printed on the back with the studio’s name, “François Hermans / Hang 421 / Rotterdam”. It was probably a family keepsake portrait taken in the studio.","A chessboard stands on a small round table; the woman on the left has her hand on a piece, while the woman on the right rests her cheek on her hand in thought. Between them a girl stands and looks straight at us. All three must have held still through the long exposure."],"spots":[["The chessboard","Board and pieces on the round table."],["The moving hand","The left-hand woman touching a piece."],["The girl","Standing behind the board, looking at us."],["The thinking woman","The woman on the right, hand to cheek."],["The footstool","A round footstool on the floor."],["The window","A latticed window at the back right."]]},"norimberg":{"title":"Draughtsman with a View of Nuremberg","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1690","place":"Germany (Nuremberg)","game":"Draughts or tables","medium":"Boxwood","size":"Diameter 5.4 cm, thickness 1.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A boxwood draughtsman carved in relief with a view of Nuremberg, its towers and castle. Above runs “SVB AQVILAE ROMANAE ALIS QVIESCO” (I rest under the wings of the Roman eagle), and below “NORIBERGA VRBIVM REGINA” (Nuremberg, queen of cities).","According to the museum’s record, the other face shows portraits of Emperor Joseph I and the seven electors. It probably carves the pride of Nuremberg, an imperial city of the Holy Roman Empire, into a gaming piece."],"spots":[["The city view","Nuremberg with its church spires and walls."],["The upper motto","“SVB AQVILAE ROMANAE…”."],["The lower motto","“NORIBERGA VRBIVM REGINA”."],["The emblem","The small device at the very top."],["The castle","The castle on the height at right."],["The rim","The stepped edge of the piece."]]},"toovenaar":{"title":"The Magician: a New Amusing Children’s Game","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1856","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Race game (a relative of the Game of the Goose)","medium":"Lithograph with letterpress text, hand-coloured","size":"44.5 × 53.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A race game titled “DE TOOVENAAR” (The Magician), “a new amusing children’s game”, with spaces 1 to 63 in a spiral. At the centre a magician stands before a table with a skull, a chalice, the moon and stars, with devilish creatures behind him; beast-headed devils fill the four corners.","The rules printed in the middle are much like the Game of the Goose. Players throw two dice: six and three on the first throw goes to 26, five and four to 53. The magician counts on like a goose; the bridge costs one, the inn costs two and a turn, the maze at 42 sends you back three, and Death makes you pay and start again."],"spots":[["The magician","The magician at his table in the centre."],["Title and rules","The title “DE TOOVENAAR” and the rules."],["Goal 63","The last space of the spiral."],["The corner devils","Beast-headed devils in the corners."],["The outer track","Picture spaces running round the edge."],["The table","Skull and chalice on the table."]]},"fichesdoos":{"title":"Ivory Box with Counters","artist":"Mariaval le Jeune","date":"1725","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Counters for card games","medium":"Ivory","size":"2 × 8 × 6 cm","credit":"","p":["A palm-sized ivory box for the counters used in place of money in card games. On the lid a crowned woman with a sceptre sits on clouds beside a peacock, with two coats of arms; with her peacock she is probably the goddess Juno.","According to the museum’s record, the lid is inscribed “Mariaval le Jeune a Rouen fecit”, and the underside shows an eagle and birds, one hanging from a gallows. Inside are nineteen large and six small counters painted with insects and flowers."],"spots":[["The crowned woman","A crowned woman seated on clouds."],["The peacock","A peacock at her feet."],["The coats of arms","Two coats of arms at upper right."],["The little bird","A bird on a perch beside her."],["The clouds","The clouds that carry her."],["The lid’s edge","The edge and clasp of the lid."]]},"vuilmere":{"title":"The Artist’s Mother Playing Checkers","artist":"Édouard Vuillard","date":"c. 1890–91","place":"France","game":"Draughts (checkers)","medium":"Pastel with graphite, stumping and erasing on ivory wove paper","size":"37.5 × 30.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A pastel by the French painter Vuillard of his mother. Vuillard lived with her and drew scenes from her home, where she worked as a dressmaker, again and again. It is signed “E Vuillard” at lower right.","Two figures in black sit across a bright orange table, and the one in front holds a draughts board. According to the museum’s title, the artist’s mother is shown here. Inside a black oval line, people and furniture melt into masses of colour and shape."],"spots":[["The board","A board of black and white squares."],["The figure behind","A figure in black seated beyond the table."],["The figure in front","The figure holding the board."],["The orange table","A blaze of colour across the picture."],["The patterned wall","A wall pattern of swirling lines."],["The signature","“E Vuillard” at lower right."]]},"dewitte":{"title":"Two Men Playing Chess","artist":"Adrien de Witte","date":"1877","place":"Belgium","game":"Chess","medium":"Probably etching","size":"13.2 × 12.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A small print by the Belgian painter and printmaker de Witte (1850–1935) of Liège. According to the museum, the literature (Clercx, 1981) supposes that the two chess players are Terme and Zeyen.","At a cloth-covered table a young man looks down at the board with folded arms, while the older man opposite rests his head on his hand in thought. Behind the board stands what looks like a box for the pieces. In the dark, finely hatched room, a quiet game unfolds."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","The board and pieces on the table."],["The box","What seems to be a box behind the board."],["The young man","Arms folded, looking down at the board."],["The older man","Head in hand, thinking."],["The tablecloth","The cloth over the table."],["The chair","The back of a bentwood chair at left."]]},"orphans":{"title":"The Orphans (Children Playing Draughts)","artist":"Charles Rolls, after W. Gill","date":"1810–1834","place":"England","game":"Draughts (checkers)","medium":"Print (engraving)","size":"22.6 × 13.9 cm","credit":"","p":["A print titled “THE ORPHANS” below, inscribed “Painted by W. Gill, Engraved by C. Rolls”. According to the museum it may have been used in The Keepsake, an annual gift book published in London.","In a kitchen, a girl and a boy play draughts on a board set on a barrel. Behind them a woman is embroidering, and at the back stands what the museum calls a loom. The title suggests the two are orphans taken in by the woman."],"spots":[["The board","The draughtboard and pieces on a barrel."],["The girl","Leaning in to watch the board."],["The boy","Moving a piece."],["The woman sewing","Embroidering behind them."],["The jug","A jug on the floor."],["The title","“THE ORPHANS” below."]]},"richelieu":{"title":"Louis XIII and Richelieu","artist":"Jan Frederik Christiaan Reckleben","date":"1850","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Chess","medium":"Steel engraving","size":"24.2 × 29.6 cm","credit":"","p":["A steel engraving by the Amsterdam printmaker Reckleben, headed “1850. NEDERLANDSCH MUSEUM. Pl. II.”. According to the museum it may have illustrated an article in the magazine Nederlandsch Museum.","Before a great fireplace, King Louis XIII of France and his minister Cardinal Richelieu play chess while a group of men looks on. The cardinal, seated at left, reaches for a piece; the king, in a plumed hat, sits back at ease. At far left a monk stands with folded arms."],"spots":[["The chessboard","Board and pieces on the draped table."],["Richelieu","The cardinal reaching for a piece."],["Louis XIII","The king in a plumed hat."],["The monk","Arms folded, watching from the left."],["The courtiers","Men gathered at the right."],["The title","“LODEWIJK DE XIII EN RICHELIEU” below."]]},"coster":{"title":"Men Playing Tric-trac by Candlelight (after De Coster)","artist":"After Adam de Coster (engraved by Vorsterman)","date":"1619–1628","place":"Flanders","game":"Tric-trac (a relative of backgammon)","medium":"Engraving","size":"37.1 × 35.1 cm","credit":"","p":["A print after a painting by the Flemish candlelight painter Adam de Coster, inscribed “A. De Coster pi.” and “Vorsterman” below. According to the museum, two men stand at a table playing tric-trac by candlelight.","At left a man with a pipe looks on; at right a girl plays the lute and a man in a cap points at the candle. The player in front, his back to us, bends over the table while his opponent holds a hand over the board. A single candle lights the scene, picking out only faces and hands."],"spots":[["The board","The board on the table, half in shadow."],["The candle","The single candle lighting the scene."],["The player from behind","Bending over the table, back turned."],["The opponent","Holding a hand over the board."],["The smoker","Watching at left, pipe in mouth."],["The lute player","A girl playing the lute at right."]]},"kiebitz":{"title":"Kiebitz (The Kibitzer)","artist":"Hans Watzek","date":"1894","place":"Probably Austria","game":"Cards","medium":"Halftone (book illustration)","size":"10.0 × 12.1 cm","credit":"","p":["A photograph by the Viennese art photographer Hans Watzek, signed “HANS WATZEK 94” at top right. It is printed as a halftone in a book whose running head reads “Is photography an art?”.","The German title “Kiebitz” means the lapwing, and is also slang for an onlooker who meddles in a card game. A man in a hat studies his cards while the man in front with a long pipe peers over. The grainy, painterly tone is typical of the art photography of the time, which sought to come close to painting."],"spots":[["The cards","The hand the man at right is studying."],["The card player","In a bowler hat, with a pipe in his mouth."],["The onlooker","The man with the long pipe, probably the “Kiebitz” of the title."],["The beer mug","A glass tankard on the table."],["The signature","“HANS WATZEK 94” at top right."],["The caption","“HANS WATZEK. — KIEBITZ.” below."]]},"evdvelde":{"title":"Tric-trac Players in a Castle Garden","artist":"Esaias van de Velde","date":"1630","place":"The Netherlands","game":"Tric-trac (a relative of backgammon)","medium":"Black chalk and grey wash","size":"18.5 × 30.1 cm","credit":"","p":["A drawing by Esaias van de Velde (1587–1630), a pioneer of Dutch genre and landscape art, signed “E.V.VELDE” at lower right. According to the museum, an elegant company plays tric-trac at a table in a castle garden.","A man stooping over the board and a seated man face each other, with women watching behind. At front left stands a wine cooler with two jugs; beyond, a boy and a peacock are by a fountain. It is a graceful afternoon of play out of doors."],"spots":[["The board","The tric-trac board on the table."],["The stooping man","In a hat, bending over the board."],["The seated man","Legs crossed, facing the board."],["The watching women","Standing behind to follow the game."],["Peacock and boy","A peacock and a boy by the fountain."],["The wine cooler","A cooler with two jugs at front left."]]},"cooghen":{"title":"Two Men Playing Tric-trac","artist":"Leendert van der Cooghen","date":"1666","place":"The Netherlands (Haarlem)","game":"Tric-trac (a relative of backgammon)","medium":"Etching","size":"9.3 × 15.6 cm","credit":"","p":["An etching by the Haarlem painter Van der Cooghen, with the year “1666” scratched on the wall above the board. According to the museum, two men sit at a table in a tavern playing tric-trac while a woman peers over the shoulder of the man at left.","The older man at left wears a fur-trimmed coat and holds a tall glass; the younger man at right wears a striking feathered hat. A little monkey sits in the window. The board’s rim and points are clearly drawn."],"spots":[["The board","The tric-trac board on the table."],["The older man","In a fur-trimmed coat."],["The glass","The tall glass in the older man’s hand."],["The woman looking on","Peering over the left man’s shoulder."],["The monkey","A little monkey in the window."],["The man in the feathered hat","The younger player in a plumed hat."]]},"roybet":{"title":"The Chess Player","artist":"Ferdinand Roybet","date":"1875","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Chess","medium":"Probably etching","size":"26.1 × 19.6 cm","credit":"","p":["A print drawn and etched by the French painter Roybet, who loved to paint seventeenth-century cavaliers (“F. Roybet del. et sc.” at lower left). Below is the title “LE JOUEUR D’ÉCHECS” and, at right, the name of the publisher Cadart.","A man in a plumed hat with a sword at his side sits sideways on a bench and reaches for the pieces. No opponent is shown; he seems to be pondering the position alone. A tankard, jug and glasses stand on the shelf behind."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","The board and pieces on the bench."],["The cavalier","The man in a plumed hat."],["The reaching hand","His hand reaching for a piece."],["The sword","A long sword at his side."],["Vessels on the shelf","Tankard, jug and glasses."],["The title","“LE JOUEUR D’ÉCHECS”."]]},"capot":{"title":"“St Peter, My Friend, You Are Capot!” (Artist and Model at Cards)","artist":"Anonymous (published by Aubert & Cie)","date":"1838","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Cards (probably piquet)","medium":"Lithograph","size":"30.5 × 22.2 cm","credit":"","p":["A lithograph used in the Paris satirical paper Le Charivari on 9 September 1838, numbered 13 in the series “Les Artistes”. The caption reads “St. Pierre, mon ami, vous êtes capot!”","According to the museum, the painter calls his card partner “Saint Peter” after the saint in the painting for which the man is modelling. Being “capot” means losing every trick in piquet, so the game is probably piquet."],"spots":[["Cards on the table","Cards played on the little table."],["The model","The long-haired man seen from behind; his hair and beard match the painted saint, so he is probably the model."],["The painter","The man at right holding his cards, probably the painter."],["The saint’s picture","The half-finished saint on the easel."],["The stool","The wooden stool the model sits on."],["The caption","“St. Pierre, mon ami, vous êtes capot!”"]]},"ziacard":{"title":"King of Clubs (Antonio Salva, Governor of Zia Pueblo)","artist":"Adam Clark Vroman (photograph), Lazarus & Melzer (publisher)","date":"1900","place":"United States (Los Angeles)","game":"Playing cards","medium":"Autotype (halftone)","size":"8.8 × 6.3 cm","credit":"","p":["According to the museum’s record, this is one card from a boxed deck of 53, joker included, printed in halftone with photographs of Native Americans. The photographs are by Adam Clark Vroman; the publisher was Lazarus & Melzer of Los Angeles.","In place of a painted king, the King of Clubs carries a portrait of Antonio Salva, governor of Zia Pueblo in New Mexico. His name and title are printed below, with K and a club in the corners. It was probably made as a souvenir for visitors to the West."],"spots":[["Corner index","K and a club at top left, as on any deck."],["The governor’s face","Antonio Salva, looking to one side."],["Long hair","Shoulder-length hair with a straight fringe."],["The neckcloth","A cloth tied at the neck of a white shirt."],["Name and title","Printed: “Antonio Salva. Governor Pueblo of Sia.”"],["Upside-down index","K and a club, reversed, at bottom right."]]},"gothink":{"title":"Playing Go","artist":"Japanese photographer (name unknown)","date":"1887–1897","place":"Japan","game":"Go","medium":"Hand-coloured photograph (book illustration)","size":"8.8 × 13 cm","credit":"","p":["Taken by an unnamed Japanese photographer and hand-coloured in Japan, this photograph is mounted on page 65 of an English-language book on Japan. The printed caption explains: “Go is a more difficult game than chess. It was introduced into Japan from China in the 8th century.”","Across a go board on legs, the woman at left places a stone while a white-bearded old man puts a hand to his head, deep in thought. The woman in the middle watches the board; in front are a tray of sweets and tea bowls. It was probably staged in a studio for foreign visitors."],"spots":[["The board","A go board on legs, with stones."],["The hand placing a stone","The woman at left reaches to the board."],["The puzzled old man","A white-bearded man with a hand to his head."],["The woman watching","In a striped kimono, in the middle."],["The tray of sweets","Sweets on a tray in front."],["The stone bowl","A lidded bowl for stones beside the board."]]},"craps":{"title":"Juvenile Gamblers","artist":"Francis A. Place","date":"1888–1893","place":"Probably the United States","game":"Dice","medium":"Photograph (book illustration)","size":"14 × 9.9 cm","credit":"","p":["According to the museum’s record, four unidentified boys play with dice; the photographer Francis A. Place is named on the mount. It is tipped in two pages before page 142 of a book and printed with the notes “Prints on American ‘Aristo’ Paper” and “made with Williams’ flash light apparatus”. It probably served as a sample for the paper and the flash.","Against a wall with straw on the floor, the boys gather round a crate. On it are a tin dish and two dice. The boy at right snaps his fingers; the one on the floor rests his chin on his hand and stares. Scratched at the bottom is “COME SEV”, probably the cry “come seven!” of craps, the American dice game."],"spots":[["Two dice","Two dice on top of the crate."],["The tin dish","A dish on the crate, probably for the stakes."],["Snapping fingers","The boy at right snaps his fingers at the dice."],["The boy on the floor","Sitting, chin on his hand, watching."],["“COME SEV”","Letters scratched below — probably “come seven!”"],["Lettering on the crate","Stencilled letters on the side of the crate."]]},"bikkel":{"title":"Study of a Man Playing Knucklebones","artist":"Jan Baptist Weenix (possibly)","date":"1642–1647","place":"Italy (Rome)","game":"Knucklebones","medium":"Black and white chalk on blue paper","size":"26.7 × 18.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A drawing in black and white chalk on blue paper. According to the museum it is possibly by the Dutch painter Weenix, made during his years in Rome; it was once sold as a work by Anthonie Palamedesz.","A man in a hat bends low, eyes on his hands, while a few small bones lie on the ground at his feet. Tossing and catching ankle bones of sheep and other animals is an ancient game, often called an ancestor of dice."],"spots":[["The face","Looking down at his hands from under the hat."],["The hands","Probably about to toss the bones."],["Bones on the ground","Small bones lying on the ground."],["The shoe","His braced foot."],["White chalk","White chalk marking the light."],["The cloak","The cloak spreading across his back."]]},"stukken":{"title":"Design for Chess Pieces","artist":"Anonymous (France)","date":"1850–1875","place":"France","game":"Chess","medium":"Pen and black ink, white and orange-red bodycolour over black chalk, on blue paper","size":"26.5 × 20.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A design drawing for a set of chessmen by an unknown French draughtsman. Two rows show five kinds of piece each: king, queen, rook, a figure piece and knight.","The upper row has a crowned king and queen and a kneeling soldier with a lance; the lower row a turbaned king, a veiled queen and a kneeling archer, so the two sides were probably meant as Western and Eastern armies. White and orange-red paint suggest carving and sheen."],"spots":[["King (top)","Crowned, holding a sceptre."],["Queen (top)","The crowned queen."],["The tower","The rook as a tower."],["The lancer","A kneeling piece with a lance."],["The turbaned king","The king of the lower row."],["The archer","A kneeling piece drawing a bow."]]},"rozetempel":{"title":"Flower Garden, or the Delightful Rose Temple (a dice game board)","artist":"Anonymous (published by Hendrik Moolenyzer)","date":"1792–1847","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Dice game board","medium":"Print","size":"46.1 × 39.6 cm","credit":"","p":["A single-sheet game board issued by the Amsterdam publisher Moolenyzer, titled at the top “BLOEMENHOF of VERMAKELIJKE ROZE TEMPEL” (Flower Garden, or the Delightful Rose Temple).","In the centre a vase of roses stands inside the “ROZE TEMPEL”, ringed by pairs of dice and flowers. It was probably played by throwing two dice and paying or collecting stakes at the flower matching the throw."],"spots":[["The title","“BLOEMENHOF of VERMAKELIJKE ROZE TEMPEL”."],["The rose temple","The “ROZE TEMPEL” in the centre."],["Dice pairs","Combinations of two dice."],["The vase of roses","Roses in a vase inside the temple."],["The tulip","One of the flowers in the ring."],["The publisher","“Te AMSTERDAM bij H. Moolenyzer”."]]},"brelan":{"title":"Le Brelan (from the Games of Childhood)","artist":"Claudine Stella (after Jacques Stella)","date":"1657","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Brelan (a three-card game)","medium":"Engraving","size":"12.0 × 14.2 cm","credit":"","p":["A plate from Les Jeux et plaisirs de l’enfance, an album of 51 scenes of children’s games. Above the verse is “LE BRELAN”; plump children sit on the ground playing cards.","The verse says one child rolls his hoop while the others, in a heap, try their luck at cards, and one who thought he would win comes away lighter. Brelan is often named among the distant ancestors of poker."],"spots":[["A hand of cards","A child fanning out his cards."],["A card on the ground","A card marked “A” on the ground."],["The onlooker","A child peering at the hand from behind."],["The hoop","A child driving a hoop with a stick."],["The tower","A round tower in the background."],["The title","“LE BRELAN” and the verse."]]},"pwkarte":{"title":"Round Playing Card with Six Parrots","artist":"Master PW of Cologne","date":"1470–1500","place":"Germany (Cologne)","game":"Playing cards (round deck)","medium":"Engraving","size":"Diameter 7.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A round playing card printed from an engraving more than five hundred years ago. According to the museum, it belongs to a deck of 70 round cards in five suits: roses, columbines, carnations, parrots and hares.","Each suit had ten cards numbered 1 to 10 and four cards showing a figure. This is the six of parrots, with an Arabic “6” at the top and a Roman “VI” at the bottom; six parrots circle the ornament in the middle."],"spots":[["“6”","The Arabic numeral at the top."],["“VI”","The Roman numeral at the bottom."],["The upper parrot","A parrot as if perched."],["The flapping parrot","A parrot with spread wings."],["The upside-down parrot","A parrot head down."],["The central ornament","The radiant ornament in the middle."]]},"nilson":{"title":"Board Games (Draughts and Tocadille)","artist":"Johann Esaias Nilson","date":"1731–1788","place":"Germany (Augsburg)","game":"Draughts and tocadille","medium":"Etching and engraving","size":"18.9 × 28.8 cm","credit":"","p":["A print in a Rococo frame designed and published by the Augsburg printmaker Nilson, titled at the top “Das Bretspiel” (The Board Game). It is the third of a set of four.","According to the museum, a man and a woman play draughts at the left table, while two men play tocadille at the right; tocadille is a game of the backgammon family. The German verse says the tocadille player is eager to win, and the draughts player needs all his senses."],"spots":[["The draughts board","On the left-hand table."],["The tocadille board","On the small table at right."],["The letter reader","A man reading a letter, pipe in mouth."],["The woman with crockery","Carrying dishes at right."],["The park view","The park beyond the ornamental frame."],["The verse","“Wer Tocadille spielt…”."]]},"chateau":{"title":"The House of Cards","artist":"François Joullain (after Charles-Antoine Coypel)","date":"1725","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Playing cards (a house of cards)","medium":"Engraving","size":"23.2 × 15.8 cm","credit":"","p":["A print engraved by Joullain after a design by the painter Coypel and published in Paris by Duchange. Coypel’s name, “CH. COYPEL”, appears on a sheet of paper lying on the floor.","A finely dressed child builds a castle of cards on a table while a boy behind watches, finger to his lips. The verse below teases card players: he who laughs at these children raising a castle at the mercy of the slightest wind would pass for wise, if he had never put cards to any other use."],"spots":[["The house of cards","Cards stacked on the table."],["Cards in hand","Cards held by the child."],["The builder","The child in a feathered cap."],["The watching boy","With a finger to his lips."],["The paper on the floor","The sheet marked “CH. COYPEL”."],["The verse","“Tel qui rit, voyant ces Enfans…”."]]},"pugillus":{"title":"A Ruined Young Man with Dice and an Elegant Woman","artist":"Jacob van der Heyden (possibly)","date":"1608–1618","place":"France (Strasbourg)","game":"Dice","medium":"Engraving","size":"9.0 × 13.2 cm","credit":"","p":["A plate from the series “Pugillus facetiarum iconographicarum” (a handful of pictorial jests) aimed at students; according to the museum it probably had 36 or more prints.","At left stands a woman in fine clothes with a fan; at right a young man in rags lifts his hat and looks at two dice and an empty purse on the ground. The captions — roughly “paradise of the eye, hell of the soul, purgatory of the purse” and “never ready cash, always torn breeches” — mock love affairs and gambling."],"spots":[["The elegant woman","In a fur hat and long gown."],["The fan","A feather fan in her hand."],["The young man","Lifting his hat as if in greeting."],["The torn clothes","His ragged doublet and breeches."],["Dice and purse","Two dice and an empty purse on the ground."],["The caption","“Nunquam bar gelt, semper zerrissen hosen”."]]},"grootebroek":{"title":"The Grootebroek Lottery","artist":"Cornelis Dusart","date":"1694–1695","place":"The Netherlands (Haarlem / Amsterdam)","game":"Lottery","medium":"Mezzotint and engraving","size":"25.1 × 19.5 cm","credit":"","p":["On 20 August 1694 a great fire struck the village of Grootebroek near Enkhuizen, and a lottery was held for its victims. This print by Dusart was published in Amsterdam by Jacob Gole.","According to the museum, a man delivers a kettle and a sealed letter to the prize winners. The caption means “showing with what joy the considerable prizes are brought home”: a trumpeter, a man hoisting the kettle and a woman throwing up her hands at the window convey the excitement of a win."],"spots":[["The sealed letter","Brought to the winners."],["The kettle","A man hoisting the prize kettle."],["The trumpet","A man blowing the news."],["The woman at the window","Raising both hands in surprise."],["The revellers","Drinking and celebrating at right."],["The title","“Lottery van Groottenbroek”."]]},"narren":{"title":"Two Fools Playing Dice (from Erasmus’s Praise of Folly)","artist":"Caspar Merian (after drawings by Hans and Ambrosius Holbein)","date":"1516 (drawings)","place":"Switzerland (Basel)","game":"Dice","medium":"Etching","size":"5.5 × 6.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A tiny illustration for Erasmus’s Praise of Folly. The Holbein brothers first drew such scenes in 1516 in the margins of a copy belonging to the scholar Oswald Myconius; Merian etched them for the edition printed in Basel in 1676.","A man in a fool’s cap with bells and another in a hood throw dice across a round table, on which three dice lie. Their bare feet complete the picture of fools carried away by gambling."],"spots":[["The dice","Three dice on the table."],["The fool in the cap","Leaning in at left."],["The bells","Bells on the tips of the cap."],["The hooded man","Facing his opponent at right."],["The hood’s bell","A bell at the end of the hood."],["Bare feet","Bare feet under the table."]]},"historiespel":{"title":"The Game of National History","artist":"Johannes Christiaan Bendorp","date":"1816","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Race game (Game of the Goose type)","medium":"Etching and engraving","size":"50.3 × 61.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A board game through Dutch history, published by Gartman in Amsterdam. According to the museum it is laid out like the Game of the Goose with 80 numbered squares, from 1 (the Batavian Revolt) to 80 (the Amsterdam school of navigation).","It adapts a 1787 version, with the events on squares 70–79 replaced; the latest event shown dates from 1816, and a separate explanation sheet belonged to the game. Battle scenes and portraits of heroes fill the squares — a way to learn history through play."],"spots":[["Start: square 1","Square 1 at bottom left."],["Goal: square 80","The navigation school in the centre."],["The title","“VADERLANDSCH HISTORIESPEL”."],["A portrait square","A square with a portrait."],["The 70s","Squares in the seventies on the inner ring."],["The publisher","“HENDRIK GARTMAN”."]]},"daumier":{"title":"Parisians Who Will Never Be Placed under Police Surveillance","artist":"Honoré Daumier","date":"1847","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Draughts","medium":"Lithograph","size":"29.7 × 22.6 cm","credit":"","p":["A lithograph by the caricaturist Daumier, used in the Paris paper Le Charivari on 25 August 1847. At the top are the series title “TOUT CE QU’ON VOUDRA” (Anything You Like) and the number 12.","At a small table in a room two elderly men play draughts while a third, chin in hand, peers at the board between them. The caption reads “Parisians who will never be placed under the surveillance of the high police” — probably a gentle joke at the expense of these harmless old men."],"spots":[["The board","The draughts board on the table."],["The player moving","At left, reaching for a piece."],["The onlooker","In the middle, chin in hand."],["The right-hand player","Staring at the board."],["The picture on the wall","A frame on the back wall."],["The caption","“Parisiens qui ne seront jamais placés…”."]]},"arnauts":{"title":"Arnauts Playing Draughts","artist":"Henry Augustin Valentin (after Jean-Léon Gérôme)","date":"1856–1886","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Draughts","medium":"Print (probably etching)","size":"21.7 × 27.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A print by Valentin after a painting by the French artist Gérôme, published in Paris by Goupil & Cie, with the title “ARNAUTES JOUANT AUX DAMES” below. Arnauts were Albanian soldiers in the service of the Ottoman Empire.","According to the museum, two Arnauts sit facing each other on a bench playing draughts. The man at left, in a pleated skirt-like costume, fiddles with something in his hands; the man at right, a long pipe in his mouth, bends over the board. Gérôme’s signature appears on the wall at left."],"spots":[["The board","The draughts board between them."],["The man at left","Seated in a pleated costume."],["His hands","Fingering something."],["The man at right","Bending over the board."],["The pipe","A long pipe in his mouth."],["The signature","Gérôme’s signature on the wall."]]},"luyken":{"title":"Turks Smoking and Playing Chess","artist":"Jan Luyken","date":"1698","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam / published in Delft)","game":"Chess","medium":"Etching and engraving","size":"30.5 × 19.1 cm","credit":"","p":["An illustration etched by Luyken for page 136 of the traveller Cornelis de Bruyn’s account of his journeys through Asia Minor (Delft, 1698). One plate carries two scenes, marked A above and B below.","In the upper scene six men in turbans smoke long pipes, and two of them play chess on the carpet; the lower scene shows four women drinking coffee and a servant. A neat row of slippers lies at the front edge of the dais."],"spots":[["The board","A chessboard on the carpet."],["The player","Reaching for a piece."],["The leaning man","Bending over the board."],["The pipes","Long pipes being smoked."],["The slippers","Shoes left in a row at the front."],["Trees in the windows","Trees outside the windows."]]},"kabinet":{"title":"Card Players","artist":"Master of the Amsterdam Cabinet","date":"1483–1487","place":"Germany","game":"Playing cards","medium":"Drypoint","size":"13.1 × 12.0 cm","credit":"","p":["An early masterpiece of drypoint — scratched straight into the copper with a needle — by the artist known as the Master of the Amsterdam Cabinet. According to the museum only four impressions survive; the others are in Coburg, Munich and Oxford.","Out of doors, a finely dressed young woman sits among three young men and a fool, a card lying in her lap. The fool and the fountain at back right suggest a garden of love. A dog sits in the foreground."],"spots":[["The young woman","A card in her lap."],["The card","On her lap."],["The youth at left","Talking to her."],["The fool","Sprawled on the ground."],["The dog","Sitting in front."],["The fountain","At back right."]]},"lievens":{"title":"Quarrelling Card Players and Death","artist":"Jan Lievens","date":"1638","place":"Flanders (Antwerp)","game":"Playing cards","medium":"Etching","size":"19.7 × 25.9 cm","credit":"","p":["An etching by Jan Lievens, once Rembrandt’s young companion, after his own painting, published in Antwerp by Frans van den Wyngaerde.","According to the museum, two card players have come to blows: the old man at right swings a jug as a weapon, his opponent has overturned the table and its cards, and a hand with a knife thrusts into the air. Death intervenes, seizing the man at left by the throat and brandishing a bone. The Latin motto below speaks of the vanity of human quarrels when death is near."],"spots":[["Death","The skeleton stepping in."],["The bone","Brandished by Death."],["The knife","Raised in the air."],["The jug","Swung by the old man."],["Cards on the floor","Scattered cards."],["The motto","“Rixas atque odia…”."]]},"sorgheloos":{"title":"The Game of Chance (from the Story of Sorgheloos)","artist":"Cornelis Anthonisz. (cut and published by Jan Ewoutsz.)","date":"1541","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Gambling at cards","medium":"Woodcut, hand-coloured","size":"39.5 × 28.9 cm","credit":"","p":["The fourth of six hand-coloured woodcuts telling the downfall of the carefree young man Sorgheloos. Name labels float above the figures, and two Dutch poems are printed below.","According to the museum, Sorgheloos, his arm around Weelde (Luxury), gambles with the pedlar Lichte Fortune (Fickle Fortune), with Ghemack (Ease) at left. At right a ragged couple, Armoede (Poverty) and Pover (Poor), walk in: luck is about to turn."],"spots":[["Cards and coins","Spread on the green table."],["Sorgheloos","The carefree young man."],["Weelde","The woman in his arm."],["Ghemack","Seated at left."],["Lichte Fortune","The pedlar with a box on his back."],["Poverty and Poor","The couple coming in at right."]]},"aleabelli":{"title":"The Game of War (Allegory of the Northern War)","artist":"Anonymous (possibly Crispijn van de Passe II)","date":"1656","place":"The Netherlands","game":"Tric-trac (a relative of backgammon)","medium":"Etching, engraving and letterpress","size":"38.8 × 35.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A satire on the Northern War of 1656, with a sheet of text in four columns pasted below. The cloth on the table is lettered “ALEA BELLI” (the gamble of war).","According to the museum, the Swede and the Dane play tric-trac, surrounded by other figures and a lance carrying the hat of “Aurea Libertas”. At right a Dutch sailor pours money into a leaking treasure chest, and in front pigs wait to be slaughtered or shipped to Sweden. In the distance are Kronborg castle and the hand of God reaching from a comet."],"spots":[["The board","The tric-trac board on the table."],["ALEA BELLI","The words on the tablecloth."],["The liberty hat","“Aurea Libertas” on the lance."],["The leaking chest","The sailor’s chest of money."],["The pigs","Pigs in the foreground."],["The comet","The comet and God’s hand at top right."]]},"shunman":{"title":"Poem Cards and their Box (surimono)","artist":"Kubota Shunman","date":"1806","place":"Japan (Edo)","game":"Poem cards (Hyakunin isshu)","medium":"Colour woodblock (surimono)","size":"16.1 × 21.3 cm","credit":"","p":["A surimono — a luxuriously printed sheet combining picture and poems, often made as a gift. It is signed by Kubota Shunman and carries poems by Nakasandō, Tago no Ishibumi and Naokoto Shibaura.","According to the museum, a few cards showing famous poets with part of their poems lie before an opened wooden box patterned with white chrysanthemums. They belong to a poem-matching game based on the Hyakunin isshu (One Hundred Poets, One Poem Each), and the three poems at upper left each refer to one of the three cards shown."],"spots":[["The chrysanthemum box","A wooden box with white chrysanthemums."],["The cards","Cards bearing poems."],["A poet card","A card showing a poet."],["The three poems","Printed at upper left."],["Signature and seal","Shunman’s signature at upper right."],["The cloth","A dark red cloth beneath."]]},"mitelli":{"title":"The Game of Trades: Who Fares Well and Who Fares Badly","artist":"Giuseppe Maria Mitelli","date":"1698","place":"Italy (Bologna)","game":"Dice game board","medium":"Etching","size":"32.6 × 43.8 cm","credit":"","p":["A dice game designed and etched by the Bolognese printmaker Mitelli. The title above says it is played with three dice, probably paying or collecting stakes according to the throw.","According to the museum, twenty squares show trades — musicians, a tobacconist, a liqueur seller, a goldsmith, a hatter, a bookseller, a lantern maker, a printer, a frame maker, a weaver, a painter and a sculptor among them — each with a short line on its fortune. There is even a square for those who keep gaming houses."],"spots":[["The title","“GIOCO DE MESTIERI…”."],["Dice throws","The dice in the musicians’ square."],["The gaming-house keepers","“Quelli che tengono gioco”."],["The tobacconist","Weighing tobacco."],["The printer","The “Stampatore” square."],["The signature","“MITELLI… 1698”."]]},"hogarth":{"title":"The Lottery","artist":"William Hogarth","date":"1721","place":"Britain (London)","game":"Lottery","medium":"Print","size":"25.9 × 32.8 cm","credit":"","p":["A print designed and made by the English satirist Hogarth, with the title “The LOTTERY” and an English explanation below; this impression comes with a handwritten Dutch translation of the text.","According to the museum, it shows the personification of National Credit, gods presenting the emblem of the national lottery, and virtues and vices connected with gambling and lots. Great lottery wheels for drawing blanks and prizes stand at left and right, while in front the lucky and the unlucky tumble together."],"spots":[["The left wheel","A great lottery wheel."],["The right wheel","The second lottery wheel."],["National Credit","The figure enthroned on the pedestal."],["The pedestal","The large central pedestal."],["The revellers","The crowd at front right."],["The title","“The LOTTERY”."]]},"matthieu":{"title":"Ladies Playing Draughts by Candlelight","artist":"Georg David Matthieu","date":"1765","place":"Germany","game":"Draughts","medium":"Etching","size":"17.9 × 22.7 cm","credit":"","p":["An etching by the German portrait painter Matthieu, signed “G. D. Matthieu 1765” at lower right. It is no tavern but a quiet evening at home.","According to the museum, three women in caps sit at a round table where a candle burns. One holds a book and watches the other two play draughts. Candle snuffers and a small box also lie on the table."],"spots":[["The board","The draughts board on the table."],["The candle","Burning in the middle of the table."],["The snuffers","For trimming the wick."],["The woman in the middle","Looking out at us."],["The woman at right","Eyes lowered, reaching for the board."],["The signature","“G. D. Matthieu 1765”."]]},"pillement":{"title":"Chinese Children Building a House of Cards (from Chinese Children’s Games)","artist":"Pierre Charles Canot (after Jean Baptiste Pillement)","date":"1759","place":"Britain (London) / France (Paris)","game":"House of cards","medium":"Etching","size":"42.6 × 27.3 cm","credit":"","p":["One of a set of eight “Recueil de plusieurs jeux d’enfants chinois”, etched by Canot after designs by Pillement, famed for his chinoiserie, and published in London and Paris. These are imaginary Chinese children of the European taste for chinoiserie.","According to the museum, in the upper scene two children build a house of cards at a round table as a third runs up with a stick; in the lower one a child rides on another’s back while a third stands at left. Both scenes are framed with fences and foliage."],"spots":[["The house of cards","On the round table."],["The child with a stick","Running up."],["The pavilion","A Chinese-style building behind."],["Piggyback","One child on another’s back."],["The standing child","At left in the lower scene."],["The publication line","“London Published… 1759”."]]},"skating":{"title":"The Skating-Rink Game","artist":"Anonymous (published by Gebroeders Koster)","date":"1870","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Race game (like the Game of the Goose)","medium":"Colour lithograph and letterpress","size":"42.6 × 57.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A board game on the theme of roller skating, published in Amsterdam by Gebroeders Koster. According to the museum it is like the Game of the Goose, with rules in Dutch, French, German and English in the four corners.","In the centre a boy and a girl on roller skates join hands, and the squares around them hold little scenes of people gliding and tumbling. Some squares show pairs of dice, and the goal is “SKATINGRINK 52”."],"spots":[["The skating pair","The boy and girl in the centre."],["The goal","“SKATINGRINK 52”."],["Dice throws","Squares marked with dice."],["Tumbling skaters","A scene of falls."],["The rules","Text in the corners."],["The publisher","“GEBROEDERS KOSTER”."]]},"gavarni":{"title":"A Wife Watching her Husband and her Lover at a Board Game","artist":"Paul Gavarni","date":"1837","place":"France (Paris)","game":"A board game (type not recorded)","medium":"Lithograph","size":"31.7 × 23.3 cm","credit":"","p":["A lithograph by the caricaturist Gavarni, used in the Paris paper Le Charivari on 20 December 1837; it is number 2 of a set of twelve.","According to the museum, a wife watches her husband and her lover play a board game. The man at left in a dressing gown and fez is probably the husband, the young man bent over the board the lover. The caption voices the lover’s complaint: spending the whole evening playing a stupid husband who wins your money, laughs at you and goes off to bed under your nose."],"spots":[["The husband","In dressing gown and fez."],["The board","On the table."],["The lover","Bent over the board."],["The wife","Standing behind."],["The ornament","A statuette under a glass dome."],["The caption","“Faire toute la soirée…”."]]},"picart":{"title":"A Lottery on a Stage (from the Great Mirror of Folly)","artist":"Bernard Picart","date":"1720–1733","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Lottery","medium":"Etching and engraving","size":"23.8 × 37.7 cm","credit":"","p":["One of the extra plates added to the Great Mirror of Folly, a collection of satires on the share mania of 1720; it is inscribed “B. Picart inv. et fec.” at lower left.","According to the museum, a lottery is held on a stage in a town square, with two boys drawing the lots from the drums. In the crowd in front, fights are breaking out. An upturned barrow, a woman with a basket of fruit and a barking dog add to the commotion."],"spots":[["The lottery drums","Large drums on the stage."],["The drawing","Figures on the stage."],["The brawl","People grappling in front."],["The fallen man","By the ladder and barrow."],["The dog","At lower right."],["The signature","“B. Picart inv. et fec.”."]]},"unigenitus":{"title":"The Game of the Goose of the Bull Unigenitus","artist":"Anonymous (after a French print)","date":"1724","place":"The Netherlands","game":"Game of the Goose","medium":"Etching and engraving","size":"46.0 × 58.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A satire on the quarrels over Pope Clement XI’s bull Unigenitus (1713), cast as a Game of the Goose. It was made in the Netherlands after an anonymous French print. The title above calls it “the Constitution game, as strange as it is unknown, muddled from head to tail and from beginning to end”.","According to the museum, the board has 63 squares, with the general rules in the middle and fourteen six-line verses on the two pilasters at the sides. Above each column are geese wearing necklaces with little crosses and bishops’ mitres."],"spots":[["The title","“Dit ’s ’t Constitutie spel…”."],["The rules","“Het Spel van de Constitutie” in the middle."],["Mitred geese (left)","Atop the left column."],["Mitred geese (right)","Atop the right column."],["The verses","On the pilasters."],["The squares","Squares with figures and scenes."]]},"bles":{"title":"Man Playing Chess (study)","artist":"David Bles","date":"1876","place":"The Netherlands","game":"Chess","medium":"Pencil, black, red and white chalk","size":"23.0 × 29.8 cm","credit":"","p":["A drawing by the Dutch genre painter David Bles, signed “DB. f” at lower left. At lower right are the words “Overwinnend Holland” (Victorious Holland) and “1796”, probably the title of a picture and the year of its scene.","A man sits deep in his chair, chin in hand, staring at the board on the table. A few pieces remain, as if the game is near its end. At right is a small sketch, apparently a second try at the same figure."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","The chessboard on the table."],["The thinking face","Chin in hand."],["The chair","The chair he sits in."],["The sketch at right","A second small sketch."],["The signature","“DB. f”."],["The inscription","“Overwinnend Holland” and 1796."]]},"visscher":{"title":"The New and Delightful Game of the Goose (with rules)","artist":"Anonymous (published by Claes Jansz. Visscher)","date":"1612–1652","place":"The Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Game of the Goose","medium":"Engraving","size":"41.9 × 47.6 cm","credit":"","p":["A Game of the Goose published in Amsterdam by Visscher, titled “HET NIEUW EN VERMAECKELYCK GHANSE-SPEL…” (the new and delightful goose game, and how to play it), with the rules printed in the margin.","According to the museum, some of the 63 squares show geese, dice, a maze, a fountain, a well and a figure of Death. The squares are linked by branch-like frames; in the central oval a company of ladies and gentlemen make music, and loving couples fill the corners."],"spots":[["The musicians","Making music in the centre."],["Goal: square 63","The 63rd square."],["Dice","A square with dice."],["The fountain","A square with a fountain."],["A goose","A square with a goose."],["The lovers","A couple at lower left."]]},"tombola":{"title":"A Tombola (from Souvenirs of the Carnival)","artist":"Paul Gavarni","date":"1840","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Tombola","medium":"Lithograph","size":"36.0 × 26.2 cm","credit":"","p":["A lithograph by Gavarni, used in the satirical paper La Caricature on 2 February 1840, headed “SOUVENIRS DU CARNAVAL”.","According to the museum, three Pierrots hold a lottery during the carnival. The Pierrot in spectacles holds up a long, thin object while masked women crowd in. The caption, “UNE TOMBOLA. Number 45 has won… a candle!”, gets its laugh from the meagre prize."],"spots":[["The raised prize","A long object held aloft."],["Pierrot in spectacles","Announcing the winner."],["The second Pierrot","In a cap at right."],["The child Pierrot","The little one in the middle."],["The masked women","Crowding in."],["The caption","“Le Nº 45 a gagné… une chandelle !”"]]},"patriotten":{"title":"The Dutch Political Dice Game (a board satirising the Patriots)","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1793","place":"The Netherlands","game":"Dice race game","medium":"Etching and engraving","size":"43.0 × 52.3 cm (open)","credit":"","p":["A folding print in the form of a board game satirising the Dutch Patriots, titled “Nêerlands Staatkundich Werp Spel” (the Netherlands’ political dice game). According to the museum it is a later state of a 1788 plate, with the year changed to 1793 and the title extended with “the French Jacobins, Sans-culottes” and more.","Opened, it shows historical scenes and satires on the Patriots in squares numbered 1 to 35, most of them reduced copies of earlier prints. The centre is a transparency: held up to the light, a Patriot appears hanged on a gallows by the devil. Along the top run provincial coats of arms and an eagle, probably that of Prussia."],"spots":[["The transparency","The centre that changes against the light."],["The eagle","Probably the Prussian eagle."],["Coats of arms","Provincial arms along the top."],["A satire square","A square holding a satire."],["The man with a gun","At lower left."],["The rider","On horseback at lower right."]]}};
